{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BwyA8QET6nBjseMenCUAJL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n2\t\n3\tfrom typing import NamedTuple\n4\t\n5\tdef report_personal_paths(\n6\t    repo_root: Path, allowlist_path: Path, fail_on_stale: bool = False,\n7\t    emit_rows: bool = False,\n8\t) -> int:\n9\t    \"\"\"Run the rule. 0 = clean, 1 = findings, 2 = unreadable input.\"\"\"\n10\t    allowlist: Dict[str, PersonalPathRow] = {}\n11\t    malformed: List[int] = []\n12\t    if not _personal_path_allowlist_is_tracked(\n13\t            repo_root, allowlist_path):\n14\t        # NOT fatal: an allowlist git cannot confirm is read as EMPTY,\n15\t        # exactly like an absent one, so every hit comes out unwaived.\n16\t        # The reason is PRINTED and not assumed — a gate that quietly\n17\t        # stops waiving looks identical to a gate that found nothing.\n18\t        # It goes to stderr under --emit-personal-path-rows, whose\n19\t        # stdout exists to be PASTED and must stay rows-only.\n20\t        print(\n21\t            \"• personal-path: %s is not tracked by git — reading it \"\n22\t            \"as EMPTY, so no row waives\"\n23\t            % _personal_path_display(allowlist_path, repo_root),\n24\t            file=(sys.stderr if emit_rows else sys.stdout),\n25\t        )\n26\t    else:\n27\t        try:\n28\t            allowlist, malformed = (\n29\t                load_personal_path_allowlist(allowlist_path))\n30\t        except PersonalPathUnreadable as exc:\n31\t            # The loader RAISES this -- for an allowlist that is a\n32\t            # directory or a device on disk, and for one git records as\n33\t            # a link -- and it is not an OSError, so it escaped the\n34\t            # tuple below as an rc-1 TRACEBACK whose frames name the\n35\t            # checkout (mechanism lane, round 10, A-P2-1; reproduced).\n36\t            # The comment on the tuple below is the post-mortem of the\n37\t            # identical bug with UnicodeDecodeError, one class earlier:\n38\t            # a fatal that is not NAMED here is a fatal that prints a\n39\t            # path. Same verdict as the scan's handler, rc 2, and the\n40\t            # message goes through the choke point because it is text\n41\t            # this module composed about a file it could not read.\n42\t            print(\n43\t                \"FATAL: cannot read %s: %s\"\n44\t                % (_personal_path_display(allowlist_path, repo_root),\n45\t                   _personal_path_safe(str(exc))),\n46\t                file=sys.stderr,\n47\t            )\n48\t            return 2\n49\t        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, re.error) as exc:\n50\t            # Fail-CLOSED on INPUT, like the terms file: an allowlist\n51\t            # that exists and cannot be read — or cannot be DECODED —\n52\t            # leaves the rule unverified, not satisfied.\n53\t            # UnicodeDecodeError is NOT an OSError, so before it was\n54\t            # named here it escaped as a traceback (rc 1, absolute\n55\t            # checkout path in the frames) instead of this sanitised\n56\t            # rc 2.\n57\t            print(\n58\t                \"FATAL: cannot read %s: %s\"\n59\t                % (_personal_path_display(allowlist_path, repo_root),\n60\t                   _personal_path_safe(_oserror_reason(exc))),\n61\t                file=sys.stderr,\n62\t            )\n63\t            return 2\n64\t    if allowlist:\n65\t        # The REASON is the only text on a green run this module did not\n66\t        # compose, and v4.1 made it PRINTED: the waived inventory groups\n67\t        # 242 files under SEVEN human sentences. The redaction\n68\t        # choke point asks whether a string names a HOME DIRECTORY; it\n69\t        # cannot ask whether it names one of RULE 1's private terms,\n70\t        # because those are compiled from the repository root and the\n71\t        # choke point has none (text lane, round 12: leaving that open was\n72\t        # judged indefensible for a gate that ships).\n73\t        #\n74\t        # It does not need to. The reasons are read in ONE place, and that\n75\t        # place is called with `repo_root`. A row whose reason names a\n76\t        # private term is MALFORMED -- refused by row number, before any\n77\t        # printer sees it -- which is the same verdict a row naming a home\n78\t        # directory already gets, from the same file, for the same reason.\n79\t        # MEASURED on the shipped allowlist: 0 of 242 reasons match.\n80\t        try:\n81\t            terms = build_pattern(repo_root)\n82\t        except Exception as exc:\n83\t            # A BOUNDARY, not a type list -- the fifth time in this file.\n84\t            # Compiling a private-terms file is arbitrary regex work: 2 000\n85\t            # open parentheses raise `RecursionError`, which is not in any\n86\t            # of the three names this clause used to carry, and emit mode\n87\t            # RETURNS before `main()`'s catch-all -- so the traceback\n88\t            # escaped with rc 1 and the checkout path in its frames\n89\t            # (mechanism lane, round 14; this repository already has a\n90\t            # probe for that input).\n91\t            # Fail-CLOSED on INPUT, like every other unreadable input here:\n92\t            # a terms file that exists and cannot be read leaves the reasons\n93\t            # unverified, not verified.\n94\t            print(\n95\t                \"FATAL: cannot read %s: %s\"\n96\t                % (_PRIVATE_TERMS_RELPATH,\n97\t                   _personal_path_safe(_oserror_reason(exc))),\n98\t                file=sys.stderr,\n99\t            )\n100\t            return 2\n101\t        # BOTH readings, for the same reason the path predicate asks both:\n102\t        # a private term spelled with full-width characters passes a raw\n103\t        # search and is then printed into the waived inventory (mechanism\n104\t        # lane, round 14).\n105\t        offenders = [\n106\t            rel for rel in sorted(allowlist)\n107\t            if (terms.search(allowlist[rel].reason)\n108\t                or terms.search(unicodedata.normalize(\n109\t                    \"NFKC\", allowlist[rel].reason)))\n110\t        ]\n111\t        for rel in offenders:\n112\t            malformed.append(allowlist[rel].rowno)\n113\t            del allowlist[rel]\n114\t        malformed.sort()\n115\t    try:\n116\t        scan = scan_personal_paths(repo_root)\n117\t    except PersonalPathUnreadable as exc:\n118\t        print(\"FATAL: cannot read in-scope file %s\"\n119\t              % _personal_path_safe(str(exc)), file=sys.stderr)\n120\t        return 2\n121\t    except OSError as exc:\n122\t        # The ENUMERATION failed, not one file: the index query the walker\n123\t        # runs, or git itself. `PersonalPathUnreadable` names the first case\n124\t        # only, so this used to escape as an UNCAUGHT exception -- and an\n125\t        # uncaught exception prints a traceback whose FRAME lines carry the\n126\t        # absolute checkout path, which is the one string this rule exists to\n127\t        # keep out of the output (text lane, land round 1; reproduced by\n128\t        # injecting PermissionError into the walker before it was believed).\n129\t        # Same sanitised exit as every other fatal in this rule.\n130\t        print(\"FATAL: cannot enumerate in-scope files: %s\"\n131\t              % _personal_path_safe(\"%s: %s\" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)),\n132\t              file=sys.stderr)\n133\t        return 2\n134\t\n135\t    verdict = personal_path_verdict(scan, allowlist, malformed)\n136\t    # FIRST, and on EVERY run: the honoured set is the same statement\n137\t    # whether the verdict is green or red, and `--emit-personal-path-rows`\n138\t    # returns before a verdict is printed at all. Its stdout must stay\n139\t    # rows-only, so its copy goes to stderr.\n140\t    _print_personal_path_honoured(\n141\t        *_personal_path_honoured_rows(verdict.waived, allowlist),\n142\t        stream=(sys.stderr if emit_rows else sys.stdout))\n143\t\n144\t    if emit_rows:\n145\t        # MALFORMED rows are not suppressed by this mode. The rows on\n146\t        # stdout are computed AGAINST the waiver set, so a waiver set the\n147\t        # parser could not read whole makes the emission itself\n148\t        # unreliable — and returning 0 here told an operator who had just\n149\t        # seen rc 1 that the problem had gone away (pair-rail round 2,\n150\t        # P2). stdout stays rows-only; the reason goes to stderr.\n151\t        rc = _emit_personal_path_rows(scan, allowlist)\n152\t        if malformed:\n153\t            print(\n154\t                \"NOTE: %d malformed allowlist row(s) — the rows above \"\n155\t                \"were computed against a waiver set that does not parse; \"\n156\t                \"fix those rows and re-run\" % len(malformed),\n157\t                file=sys.stderr)\n158\t            return 1\n159\t        return rc\n160\t\n161\t    if verdict.stale:\n162\t        # Advisory by design: a row waives ONE path, so a row matching nothing\n163\t        # waives nothing. Fatal-by-default would turn every archived plan —\n164\t        # and every adopter checkout, which has none of these artifacts — red.\n165\t        print(\"• personal-path: %d allowlist row(s) match no file (stale)\"\n166\t              % len(verdict.stale))\n167\t\n168\t    if not (verdict.unwaived or verdict.malformed or verdict.mismatched):\n169\t        if fail_on_stale and verdict.stale:\n170\t            print(\"❌ personal-path: stale allowlist rows, with --fail-on-stale\")\n171\t            # MASKED here, because the printer no longer escapes: the\n172\t            # stale list is the one place that handed it raw rels.\n173\t            _print_personal_path_group(\n174\t                \"STALE\",\n175\t                [_personal_path_mask_rel(rel) for rel in verdict.stale])\n176\t            return 1\n177\t        print(\"✓ personal-path: no absolute home paths outside the allowlist \"\n178\t              \"under docs/ or .claude/plans/ (%d row(s) honoured, above)\"\n179\t              % len(verdict.waived))\n180\t        return 0\n181\t\n182\t    print(\"❌ personal-path: absolute home path(s) with no allowlist row\")\n183\t    rows: List[str] = []\n184\t    name_hits = False\n185\t    for hit in verdict.unwaived:\n186\t        for lineno in hit.lines:\n187\t            if lineno == _PERSONAL_PATH_NAME_LINE:\n188\t                name_hits = True\n189\t            rows.append(\"%s:%d\"\n190\t                        % (_personal_path_mask_rel(hit.rel), lineno))\n191\t    _print_personal_path_group(\"file:line\", rows)\n192\t    _print_personal_path_group(\n193\t        \"row sha256 no longer matches the file (re-read it, then record \"\n194\t        \"the new digest)\",\n195\t        [_personal_path_mask_rel(rel) for rel in verdict.mismatched],\n196\t    )\n197\t    _report_personal_path_eol_suspicion(scan, allowlist, verdict)\n198\t    if name_hits:\n199\t        print(\"    (`:0` = the PATH itself names a home directory. Rename\")\n200\t        print(\"     the file -- an allowlist row naming one is refused.)\")\n201\t    # Row NUMBERS, composed here: nothing of the file's content reaches\n202\t    # this list, which is why it needs no masking now that the printer\n203\t    # does none.\n204\t    _print_personal_path_group(\n205\t        \"MALFORMED allowlist rows (expected `<path> | sha256:<64 hex> | \"\n206\t        \"<reason>`)\",\n207\t        [\"row %d\" % rowno for rowno in verdict.malformed],\n208\t    )\n209\t    print(\"\")\n210\t    print(\"Cure, in order of preference:\")\n211\t    print(\"  1. remove the absolute path — a placeholder (/Users/<user>/, \"\n212\t          \"$HOME, ~) says the same thing;\")\n213\t    print(\"  2. if the file is a FROZEN artifact that must not be rewritten, \"\n214\t          \"add ONE row per file to\")\n215\t    print(\"     %s\" % _personal_path_display(allowlist_path, repo_root))\n216\t    print(\"     in the form `<path> | sha256:<64 hex> | <reason>`, and\")\n217\t    print(\"     COMMIT IT IN THE SAME CHANGE so the waiver is reviewable\")\n218\t    print(\"     — an allowlist git does not TRACK is read as empty. The\")\n219\t    print(\"     digest pins the row to the bytes you read: edit the file\")\n220\t    print(\"     and the waiver lapses, on purpose. Rows to start from,\")\n221\t    print(\"     digests already filled in:\")\n222\t    print(\"       python3 .claude/scripts/check_contamination.py \"\n223\t          \"--emit-personal-path-rows\")\n224\t    print(\"NOTHING is exempt by NAME, by SUFFIX or by SIGNATURE — not\")\n225\t    print(\"OWNER-*.sh, not *.asc, not approved.md. Every in-scope file is\")\n226\t    print(\"scanned the same way, and the ONLY waiver is a row pinned to\")\n227\t    print(\"the sha256 of the content it waives. A signed sentinel is\")\n228\t    print(\"waived by such a row like anything else: the signature freezes\")\n229\t    print(\"the bytes, so the digest cannot lapse behind your back.\")\n230\t    return 1\n231\t\n232\t\n233\t\n234\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50141584-ed08-437c-bb91-f56d610ea253","uuid":"02b7756c-7575-420a-a28a-542fc1f4fe58","timestamp":"2026-11-13T18:36:34.040Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_contamination/personal_reporting.py","content":"from __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom typing import NamedTuple\n\ndef report_personal_paths(\n    repo_root: Path, allowlist_path: Path, fail_on_stale: bool = False,\n    emit_rows: bool = False,\n) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Run the rule. 0 = clean, 1 = findings, 2 = unreadable input.\"\"\"\n    allowlist: Dict[str, PersonalPathRow] = {}\n    malformed: List[int] = []\n    if not _personal_path_allowlist_is_tracked(\n            repo_root, allowlist_path):\n        # NOT fatal: an allowlist git cannot confirm is read as EMPTY,\n        # exactly like an absent one, so every hit comes out unwaived.\n        # The reason is PRINTED and not assumed — a gate that quietly\n        # stops waiving looks identical to a gate that found nothing.\n        # It goes to stderr under --emit-personal-path-rows, whose\n        # stdout exists to be PASTED and must stay rows-only.\n        print(\n            \"• personal-path: %s is not tracked by git — reading it \"\n            \"as EMPTY, so no row waives\"\n            % _personal_path_display(allowlist_path, repo_root),\n            file=(sys.stderr if emit_rows else sys.stdout),\n        )\n    else:\n        try:\n            allowlist, malformed = (\n                load_personal_path_allowlist(allowlist_path))\n        except PersonalPathUnreadable as exc:\n            # The loader RAISES this -- for an allowlist that is a\n            # directory or a device on disk, and for one git records as\n            # a link -- and it is not an OSError, so it escaped the\n            # tuple below as an rc-1 TRACEBACK whose frames name the\n            # checkout (mechanism lane, round 10, A-P2-1; reproduced).\n            # The comment on the tuple below is the post-mortem of the\n            # identical bug with UnicodeDecodeError, one class earlier:\n            # a fatal that is not NAMED here is a fatal that prints a\n            # path. Same verdict as the scan's handler, rc 2, and the\n            # message goes through the choke point because it is text\n            # this module composed about a file it could not read.\n            print(\n                \"FATAL: cannot read %s: %s\"\n                % (_personal_path_display(allowlist_path, repo_root),\n                   _personal_path_safe(str(exc))),\n                file=sys.stderr,\n            )\n            return 2\n        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, re.error) as exc:\n            # Fail-CLOSED on INPUT, like the terms file: an allowlist\n            # that exists and cannot be read — or cannot be DECODED —\n            # leaves the rule unverified, not satisfied.\n            # UnicodeDecodeError is NOT an OSError, so before it was\n            # named here it escaped as a traceback (rc 1, absolute\n            # checkout path in the frames) instead of this sanitised\n            # rc 2.\n            print(\n                \"FATAL: cannot read %s: %s\"\n                % (_personal_path_display(allowlist_path, repo_root),\n                   _personal_path_safe(_oserror_reason(exc))),\n                file=sys.stderr,\n            )\n            return 2\n    if allowlist:\n        # The REASON is the only text on a green run this module did not\n        # compose, and v4.1 made it PRINTED: the waived inventory groups\n        # 242 files under SEVEN human sentences. The redaction\n        # choke point asks whether a string names a HOME DIRECTORY; it\n        # cannot ask whether it names one of RULE 1's private terms,\n        # because those are compiled from the repository root and the\n        # choke point has none (text lane, round 12: leaving that open was\n        # judged indefensible for a gate that ships).\n        #\n        # It does not need to. The reasons are read in ONE place, and that\n        # place is called with `repo_root`. A row whose reason names a\n        # private term is MALFORMED -- refused by row number, before any\n        # printer sees it -- which is the same verdict a row naming a home\n        # directory already gets, from the same file, for the same reason.\n        # MEASURED on the shipped allowlist: 0 of 242 reasons match.\n        try:\n            terms = build_pattern(repo_root)\n        except Exception as exc:\n            # A BOUNDARY, not a type list -- the fifth time in this file.\n            # Compiling a private-terms file is arbitrary regex work: 2 000\n            # open parentheses raise `RecursionError`, which is not in any\n            # of the three names this clause used to carry, and emit mode\n            # RETURNS before `main()`'s catch-all -- so the traceback\n            # escaped with rc 1 and the checkout path in its frames\n            # (mechanism lane, round 14; this repository already has a\n            # probe for that input).\n            # Fail-CLOSED on INPUT, like every other unreadable input here:\n            # a terms file that exists and cannot be read leaves the reasons\n            # unverified, not verified.\n            print(\n                \"FATAL: cannot read %s: %s\"\n                % (_PRIVATE_TERMS_RELPATH,\n                   _personal_path_safe(_oserror_reason(exc))),\n                file=sys.stderr,\n            )\n            return 2\n        # BOTH readings, for the same reason the path predicate asks both:\n        # a private term spelled with full-width characters passes a raw\n        # search and is then printed into the waived inventory (mechanism\n        # lane, round 14).\n        offenders = [\n            rel for rel in sorted(allowlist)\n            if (terms.search(allowlist[rel].reason)\n                or terms.search(unicodedata.normalize(\n                    \"NFKC\", allowlist[rel].reason)))\n        ]\n        for rel in offenders:\n            malformed.append(allowlist[rel].rowno)\n            del allowlist[rel]\n        malformed.sort()\n    try:\n        scan = scan_personal_paths(repo_root)\n    except PersonalPathUnreadable as exc:\n        print(\"FATAL: cannot read in-scope file %s\"\n              % _personal_path_safe(str(exc)), file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n    except OSError as exc:\n        # The ENUMERATION failed, not one file: the index query the walker\n        # runs, or git itself. `PersonalPathUnreadable` names the first case\n        # only, so this used to escape as an UNCAUGHT exception -- and an\n        # uncaught exception prints a traceback whose FRAME lines carry the\n        # absolute checkout path, which is the one string this rule exists to\n        # keep out of the output (text lane, land round 1; reproduced by\n        # injecting PermissionError into the walker before it was believed).\n        # Same sanitised exit as every other fatal in this rule.\n        print(\"FATAL: cannot enumerate in-scope files: %s\"\n              % _personal_path_safe(\"%s: %s\" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)),\n              file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n\n    verdict = personal_path_verdict(scan, allowlist, malformed)\n    # FIRST, and on EVERY run: the honoured set is the same statement\n    # whether the verdict is green or red, and `--emit-personal-path-rows`\n    # returns before a verdict is printed at all. Its stdout must stay\n    # rows-only, so its copy goes to stderr.\n    _print_personal_path_honoured(\n        *_personal_path_honoured_rows(verdict.waived, allowlist),\n        stream=(sys.stderr if emit_rows else sys.stdout))\n\n    if emit_rows:\n        # MALFORMED rows are not suppressed by this mode. The rows on\n        # stdout are computed AGAINST the waiver set, so a waiver set the\n        # parser could not read whole makes the emission itself\n        # unreliable — and returning 0 here told an operator who had just\n        # seen rc 1 that the problem had gone away (pair-rail round 2,\n        # P2). stdout stays rows-only; the reason goes to stderr.\n        rc = _emit_personal_path_rows(scan, allowlist)\n        if malformed:\n            print(\n                \"NOTE: %d malformed allowlist row(s) — the rows above \"\n                \"were computed against a waiver set that does not parse; \"\n                \"fix those rows and re-run\" % len(malformed),\n                file=sys.stderr)\n            return 1\n        return rc\n\n    if verdict.stale:\n        # Advisory by design: a row waives ONE path, so a row matching nothing\n        # waives nothing. Fatal-by-default would turn every archived plan —\n        # and every adopter checkout, which has none of these artifacts — red.\n        print(\"• personal-path: %d allowlist row(s) match no file (stale)\"\n              % len(verdict.stale))\n\n    if not (verdict.unwaived or verdict.malformed or verdict.mismatched):\n        if fail_on_stale and verdict.stale:\n            print(\"❌ personal-path: stale allowlist rows, with --fail-on-stale\")\n            # MASKED here, because the printer no longer escapes: the\n            # stale list is the one place that handed it raw rels.\n            _print_personal_path_group(\n                \"STALE\",\n                [_personal_path_mask_rel(rel) for rel in verdict.stale])\n            return 1\n        print(\"✓ personal-path: no absolute home paths outside the allowlist \"\n              \"under docs/ or .claude/plans/ (%d row(s) honoured, above)\"\n              % len(verdict.waived))\n        return 0\n\n    print(\"❌ personal-path: absolute home path(s) with no allowlist row\")\n    rows: List[str] = []\n    name_hits = False\n    for hit in verdict.unwaived:\n        for lineno in hit.lines:\n            if lineno == _PERSONAL_PATH_NAME_LINE:\n                name_hits = True\n            rows.append(\"%s:%d\"\n                        % (_personal_path_mask_rel(hit.rel), lineno))\n    _print_personal_path_group(\"file:line\", rows)\n    _print_personal_path_group(\n        \"row sha256 no longer matches the file (re-read it, then record \"\n        \"the new digest)\",\n        [_personal_path_mask_rel(rel) for rel in verdict.mismatched],\n    )\n    _report_personal_path_eol_suspicion(scan, allowlist, verdict)\n    if name_hits:\n        print(\"    (`:0` = the PATH itself names a home directory. Rename\")\n        print(\"     the file -- an allowlist row naming one is refused.)\")\n    # Row NUMBERS, composed here: nothing of the file's content reaches\n    # this list, which is why it needs no masking now that the printer\n    # does none.\n    _print_personal_path_group(\n        \"MALFORMED allowlist rows (expected `<path> | sha256:<64 hex> | \"\n        \"<reason>`)\",\n        [\"row %d\" % rowno for rowno in verdict.malformed],\n    )\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"Cure, in order of preference:\")\n    print(\"  1. remove the absolute path — a placeholder (/Users/<user>/, \"\n          \"$HOME, ~) says the same thing;\")\n    print(\"  2. if the file is a FROZEN artifact that must not be rewritten, \"\n          \"add ONE row per file to\")\n    print(\"     %s\" % _personal_path_display(allowlist_path, repo_root))\n    print(\"     in the form `<path> | sha256:<64 hex> | <reason>`, and\")\n    print(\"     COMMIT IT IN THE SAME CHANGE so the waiver is reviewable\")\n    print(\"     — an allowlist git does not TRACK is read as empty. The\")\n    print(\"     digest pins the row to the bytes you read: edit the file\")\n    print(\"     and the waiver lapses, on purpose. Rows to start from,\")\n    print(\"     digests already filled in:\")\n    print(\"       python3 .claude/scripts/check_contamination.py \"\n          \"--emit-personal-path-rows\")\n    print(\"NOTHING is exempt by NAME, by SUFFIX or by SIGNATURE — not\")\n    print(\"OWNER-*.sh, not *.asc, not approved.md. Every in-scope file is\")\n    print(\"scanned the same way, and the ONLY waiver is a row pinned to\")\n    print(\"the sha256 of the content it waives. A signed sentinel is\")\n    print(\"waived by such a row like anything else: the signature freezes\")\n    print(\"the bytes, so the digest cannot lapse behind your back.\")\n    return 1\n\n\n\n","numLines":234,"startLine":1,"totalLines":234}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BwyA8QET6nBjseMenCUAJL","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch1QnvCZ5MgbCwb64WMAH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012yA1yG9tGzEZKoMzv7FNhR","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_contamination/personal_constants.py"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15027,"cache_read_input_tokens":99988,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15027},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50141584-ed08-437c-bb91-f56d610ea253","uuid":"09ed277d-2743-4ea1-92bd-000dfe82737a","timestamp":"2026-11-13T18:36:35.579Z","request_id":"req_011Ch1Qnuz9SEzfST93aC1ys","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_012yA1yG9tGzEZKoMzv7FNhR":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_contamination/personal_constants.py"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012yA1yG9tGzEZKoMzv7FNhR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"Rule 2 constants and policy rationale for the contamination CLI.\"\"\"\n2\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n3\t\n4\timport errno\n5\timport os\n6\timport re\n7\timport stat\n8\timport time\n9\t\n10\t# RULE 2 — `personal-path`: absolute home paths under docs/ and .claude/plans/\n11\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n12\t# The TERM rule above exempts those two trees almost entirely: `.claude/plans/*`\n13\t# is a WHOLESALE glob (fnmatch's `*` crosses `/`) and most of `docs/` sits on\n14\t# the exact allowlist. Both exemptions were written for PROSE that legitimately\n15\t# names adopter context; neither was ever a decision about absolute HOME paths.\n16\t# This repository is PUBLIC, and the plan tree is where frozen rail transcripts\n17\t# and ceremony logs land — which is exactly where a `/Users/<name>/` leaks.\n18\t# PLAN-186 §Riscos recorded the follow-up (Codex P1, S339); this rule is it.\n19\t#\n20\t# WHAT IT FLAGS: an absolute home directory whose owner segment is a REAL name\n21\t# — `/Users/<name>/...`, `/home/<name>/...`, and the harness slug form\n22\t# `-Users-<name>-...` that a project state directory carries. The documented\n23\t# placeholder style (`/Users/<user>/`, `/Users/<user>/`, `/home/<user>/`) is\n24\t# not a hit: `<` is not a path character, and the generic owner segments are\n25\t# listed below.\n26\t#\n27\t# NOTHING IS EXEMPT BY NAME OR BY SUFFIX. Every in-scope tracked path is\n28\t# read and matched the SAME WAY -- no basename, no extension and no\n29\t# directory decides that a file is not looked at. Three versions of this\n30\t# pack shipped a name-shaped skip and the pair-rail found each of them:\n31\t# `*.asc` exempt by suffix (v2, land round 1), `*-approved.md` exempt by\n32\t# basename (v2, round cure-2), and `OWNER-*.sh` exempt by basename BEFORE\n33\t# any content was read (v3, land round 1 -- the finding that dropped the\n34\t# pack). Each time the bypass was one rename: a tracked symlink\n35\t# `docs/OWNER-leak.sh -> /Users/<owner>/repo` walked through the gate that\n36\t# `docs/plain-leak.md` with the SAME target failed. The class is closed by\n37\t# REMOVAL, not by enumerating the safe names -- which is the only closure\n38\t# this repository has ever made stick (CLAUDE.md, S336 lesson).\n39\t#\n40\t# WAIVERS therefore act on a hit that has ALREADY been found, and come\n41\t# from exactly ONE place:\n42\t#   * the tracked allowlist file, ONE `<path> | sha256:<64 hex> | <reason>`\n43\t#     row per file. A row is a human decision with the decision written\n44\t#     down, and BOTH halves are checked: the reason must contain a\n45\t#     character that is actually shown and none that is a control or a\n46\t#     format character, so a reason of zero-width spaces -- which\n47\t#     satisfied the grammar and waived a real leak (round 19) -- is\n48\t#     MALFORMED. There is no glob, no directory prefix and no generated\n49\t#     baseline, because every shape that waives a file nobody named is the\n50\t#     shape this rule exists to remove. THE ROW IS PINNED TO CONTENT: it\n51\t#     waives the file only while the bytes the scan read hash to the\n52\t#     recorded digest, so a row cannot waive a file FOREVER (pair-rail,\n53\t#     land round 1, P2) -- one added byte and the waiver lapses, the gate\n54\t#     goes red, and a human re-reads the file and re-records the digest.\n55\t#     TRACKEDNESS IS VERIFIED, not assumed: an allowlist git cannot\n56\t#     confirm is read as EMPTY, so a `touch`ed copy waives nothing\n57\t#     (`_personal_path_allowlist_is_tracked`).\n58\t#\n59\t# THERE IS NO SECOND WAIVER, AND NO RULE WAIVER AT ALL. A signed\n60\t# sentinel (`approved.md` / `*-approved.md`) used to be forgiven by a\n61\t# RULE that asked four questions of content -- a sibling `.asc`, an\n62\t# armoured signature body over a size floor, an `Approved-By`\n63\t# fingerprint a tracked row granted, and not-a-symlink. Every one of\n64\t# those answers is TRANSPLANTABLE by anyone who can write a file: the\n65\t# sidecar is not bound to the document, and the Owner's fingerprint is\n66\t# published. So `docs/leak-approved.md` with a copied `.asc` was waived\n67\t# while the same bytes under another name were a finding -- exemption\n68\t# by NAME in a content-shaped costume, which is the one class this rule\n69\t# exists to remove. It waived ZERO hits on this tree (CENSUS below), so\n70\t# deleting it moved no row and closed the surface outright.\n71\t#\n72\t# A signature this gate can actually VERIFY would be a second waiver\n73\t# earned by content; that needs `gpg` and a keyring, and it is\n74\t# `.claude/plans/PLAN-186-FOLLOWUP-sentinel-gpg-verification.md`. Until\n75\t# it lands, a frozen signed artifact carrying a home path is waived the\n76\t# same way everything else is: by a row pinned to its digest -- and a\n77\t# signature freezes that digest, so the row cannot go stale behind\n78\t# anyone's back.\n79\t#\n80\t# The rule NEVER prints a matched LINE — its findings land in a PUBLIC CI log,\n81\t# and a gate that quotes the leak it found is a second copy of it. It prints\n82\t# `file:line`, which is what a human needs to go and look.\n83\t#\n84\t# THAT RULE APPLIES TO THE GATE'S OWN OUTPUT, at one choke point. Everything\n85\t# this rule prints that it did not compose itself — a waiver REASON, an\n86\t# allowlist path outside the repo, an ignored CLI token, the REASON an\n87\t# OSError carries — goes through `_personal_path_safe`, which runs the\n88\t# rule's own predicate over the string and replaces it wholesale when it\n89\t# names a home directory. Two of those fatal prints were interpolating\n90\t# `_oserror_reason(exc)` RAW until the text lane of round 20 injected an\n91\t# error whose diagnostic carried a home path and watched it print at rc 2;\n92\t# an exhaustive-sounding sentence beside four safe call sites is how the\n93\t# fifth stays unnoticed. There is ONE deliberate exception and it is not a\n94\t# print: the row offered by `--emit-personal-path-rows`, which the parser\n95\t# has to read back, is built from `hit.rel` under two explicit refusals --\n96\t# see `_personal_path_safe`. Four separate\n97\t# print sites were found leaking by the pair-rail (round cure-1, P2 x3); they\n98\t# were four instances of one shape, so the cure is one predicate at the\n99\t# boundary, not four redactions. A contaminated waiver reason is ALSO refused\n100\t# at parse time, so it never reaches a printer at all.\n101\t#\n102\t# A tracked SYMLINK is scanned as what git stores: the TARGET STRING. Not\n103\t# doing so was a false negative with a one-line trigger (rail round cure-1,\n104\t# P1) — `is_file()` follows the link, so a dangling link read as \"absent\" and\n105\t# a resolvable one had someone else's file scanned in its place, while the\n106\t# `/Users/<name>/…` in the committed link payload was never looked at.\n107\t#\n108\t# FAIL-CLOSED ON INPUT (CLAUDE.md §4): an allowlist that exists and cannot be\n109\t# read or DECODED (a single non-UTF-8 byte used to raise through the CLI as a\n110\t# traceback carrying the absolute checkout path — rail round cure-1, P2), and\n111\t# an in-scope file that exists and cannot be read, are both fatal\n112\t# (rc 2) — an unverified scan is not a clean one. That fatal names the file the\n113\t# way every other line of this rule does — repo-relative, NEVER absolute:\n114\t# `str(OSError)` embeds `filename`, and on an operator machine that filename is\n115\t# the very `/Users/<name>/...` this rule exists to keep out of a public log\n116\t# (`_oserror_reason` below is what strips it; measured, cured,\n117\t# controlled). A file that is TRACKED but\n118\t# ABSENT from the worktree is skipped: absence is not unreadable input. Bytes\n119\t# that are not valid UTF-8 are REPLACED, never skipped, so \"the guard could not\n120\t# parse it\" cannot read as \"the guard found nothing\".\n121\t#\n122\t# DECLARED BLIND SPOTS (a rule that names what it cannot see):\n123\t#   * a path split across two lines, or assembled from fragments;\n124\t#   * the Windows form, and the `-home-<name>-` slug form (the latter would\n125\t#     fire on ordinary hyphenated prose such as `-home-page-`);\n126\t#   * a HYPHENATED owner in the SLUG form only: `-Users-<user>-jane-repo` reads\n127\t#     as owner `dev`, a placeholder, and is missed (pair-rail round cure-2,\n128\t#     P2 — DECLARED, not cured). Reading further would mean treating\n129\t#     `-Users-<user>-work-repo` as owner `runner-work`, which is not on the\n130\t#     placeholder list, so the fail-closed reading fires on the documented\n131\t#     placeholder style the rule exists to protect. The direct form\n132\t#     `/Users/<user>/` IS caught (that regex accepts `-`), and a document\n133\t#     carrying the slug almost always carries the direct form too;\n134\t#   * a relative `Users/<name>/...` with no leading slash;\n135\t#   * a personal path glued straight onto a preceding word character\n136\t#     (`x/Users/<name>`) — the price of not firing on every `.../home/<page>`\n137\t#     URL route, a shape docs legitimately carry;\n138\t#   * TWO undecodable bytes in one line: the second reading removes every\n139\t#     U+FFFD at once, so `/Us<FF>ers/<FF>name` is covered, but a byte that\n140\t#     decodes to a VALID character (rather than to U+FFFD) is not modelled at\n141\t#     all — this rule is a leak detector, not an adversary-proof filter;\n142\t#   * THE INDEX IS ENUMERATED AND THE WORKTREE IS READ. `git ls-files`\n143\t#     names the entries; the bytes come from the filesystem. Stage a\n144\t#     leaking blob and then overwrite or delete it in the worktree and\n145\t#     the scan sees the clean copy, while `git commit` lands the staged\n146\t#     one -- and a row pins the WORKTREE bytes, not necessarily the bytes\n147\t#     being landed (pair-rail round 1, P1, both lanes). DECLARED, not\n148\t#     cured: closing it means reading blobs through `git cat-file`\n149\t#     instead of the filesystem, which changes what every digest in the\n150\t#     shipped allowlist means. It is also the pre-existing behaviour of\n151\t#     RULE 1 and of `FileWalker`, and in CI -- where this gate runs -- the\n152\t#     index and the worktree are the same tree by construction;\n153\t#   * the owner class is a DELIMITER class, so an owner segment that\n154\t#     CONTAINS one of the delimiters (`<`, `>`, a quote, a comma, a\n155\t#     closing bracket) is still read short. That set is exactly what\n156\t#     keeps `/Users/<user>/` clean without a special case, and the trade\n157\t#     is deliberate: the alternative fires on the documented placeholder\n158\t#     style this rule exists to encourage;\n159\t#   * `/./Users/<name>/...` is missed -- the lookbehind rejects a leading\n160\t#     `.`, which is what keeps the rule off `../home/x` and\n161\t#     `example.com/home/index`. Same family as the relative form above;\n162\t#   * a row waives the WHOLE file AT ONE DIGEST: two leaks added in the\n163\t#     same edit are waived together by the row that records that edit's\n164\t#     sha256. What a row can no longer do is waive the NEXT edit -- the\n165\t#     digest is the thing a reviewer re-approves;\n166\t#   * a FROZEN artifact is waived by a row like anything else. There is\n167\t#     no rule waiver to earn, so the operator cost of a signed sentinel\n168\t#     that really does carry a home path is one row -- and because a\n169\t#     signature freezes the bytes, that row's digest cannot lapse\n170\t#     without the signature lapsing first;\n171\t#   * a symlink's TARGET is scanned as text, so a link to a file whose\n172\t#     CONTENT carries a personal path is judged by the link, not by that\n173\t#     file — the file itself is scanned on its own if it is tracked and in\n174\t#     scope, and out of scope it was never this rule's subject;\n175\t#   * TRACKED is not COMMITTED. The allowlist's trackedness is\n176\t#     verified, so a file git never heard of waives nothing — but an\n177\t#     UNCOMMITTED edit to a tracked allowlist still grants. That edit\n178\t#     is a `git diff`, which is the reviewable artifact the claim was\n179\t#     always about, and refusing it would make \"add the row, run the\n180\t#     gate, commit it in the same change\" impossible in that order.\n181\t#\n182\t# CENSUS — the table most \"MEASURED\" notes below are derived from. Two are\n183\t# not, and say so where they stand: the case-variant fold and the NUL\n184\t# refusal each name their own instrument, because each counts a different\n185\t# population than this table does. A note that cites this table when it\n186\t# measured something else would be the drift this block exists to stop.\n187\t#\n188\t# It is the SOURCE, not the only occurrence: a note repeats a figure\n189\t# from it inline (the 242 waived files) because the\n190\t# sentence is unreadable without it. Each says CENSUS beside the number\n191\t# so a reader knows where it came from, and a claim of exclusivity that\n192\t# the file itself contradicts is worse than none (pair-rail round 3, P3).\n193\t#\n194\t# Every \"MEASURED\" note below cites this table instead of carrying a figure\n195\t# of its own. Three of them had already drifted by the time a cross-vendor\n196\t# review read the module (S344): two docstrings were a generation behind the\n197\t# disk, while sibling comments in this same file carried the current ones. A\n198\t# number written in six places is six chances to be wrong; written once,\n199\t# with the command beside it, it is one thing to re-run.\n200\t#\n201\t# Measured 2026-09-04 (S344) at 056e11c, on the tree with this rule applied\n202\t# and staged. From the repository root:\n203\t#\n204\t#   git ls-files -z | tr '\\0' '\\n' | grep -c .                      -> 5222\n205\t#   git ls-files 'docs/*' '.claude/plans/*' | wc -l                 -> 2171\n206\t#   git ls-files 'docs/*' '.claude/plans/*' \\\n207\t#     | grep -cE '(^|/)(approved|.*-approved)\\.md$'                 ->   75\n208\t#   ... the same list, counting those with `test -f \"$f.asc\"`       ->   75\n209\t#   git ls-files 'docs/*' '.claude/plans/*' | grep -c '\\.asc$'      ->   82\n210\t#\n211\t# The rest is this module's own predicates driven over `git ls-files`, plus\n212\t# one `python3 .claude/scripts/check_contamination.py` for the verdict line:\n213\t#\n214\t#   * in-scope `.asc`: 0 are symlinks, 0 carry a hit line, and every one\n215\t#     decodes to the same 147-byte signature body;\n216\t#   * in-scope sentinels: 75, and NONE of them carries a hit. That is the\n217\t#     number that made the DELETION of the rule waiver free: 34 of them\n218\t#     passed the four checks the deleted rule asked, and not one of those\n219\t#     34 had anything to be forgiven. A waiver that forgives nothing is\n220\t#     an attack surface with no user;\n221\t#   * in-scope files carrying a hit: 242 — each one waived by a row whose\n222\t#     digest matches, with 0 unwaived, 0 digest mismatches, 0 stale and\n223\t#     0 malformed rows;\n224\t#   * what the REMOVED name exemptions were hiding, measured on this tree\n225\t#     before they were removed: of 54 in-scope `OWNER-*.sh` scripts, 0\n226\t#     carry a hit (they name the checkout through `$HOME` and `$CEO_REPO`,\n227\t#     never literally); of 75 in-scope sentinels, 0 carry a hit; 0 in-scope\n228\t#     paths are symlinks; 0 in-scope regular files carry a binary suffix.\n229\t#     EVERY exemption this rule ever had waived exactly nothing — so the\n230\t#     uniform scan and the deleted `_SKIP_SUFFIXES` branch move ZERO rows,\n231\t#     and the waived count is the same 242 before and after;\n232\t#   * in-scope paths hitting on their own NAME: 0. Tracked paths that are\n233\t#     undecodable in scope: 0;\n234\t#   * hit lines over every tracked file: 4238 under the widened `\\w` pair\n235\t#     and 4237 under the ASCII one. The ONE line they disagree on is the\n236\t#     non-ASCII example spelled out in the `_PERSONAL_PATH_HOME_RE` note\n237\t#     below — a line under `.claude/scripts/`, which this rule never scans,\n238\t#     so the widening moves no ROW.\n239\t#\n240\t# These figures AGE with the tree; they are dated and pinned to a base so a\n241\t# reader can tell staleness from drift. Re-run the commands before quoting\n242\t# them, and change them HERE — not in the prose that cites them.\n243\t\n244\t#: Only these two trees. The rule deliberately does NOT widen the walk — the\n245\t#: term rule already sees everything else.\n246\t_PERSONAL_PATH_SCOPE = (\".claude/plans/\", \"docs/\")\n247\t\n248\t#: `/Users/<seg>` and `/home/<seg>`. The lookbehind keeps the rule off URL\n249\t#: routes and nested directories (`example.com/home/index`, `/srv/home/x`):\n250\t#: a home path is written from a path root, so it is preceded by `/`, a quote,\n251\t#: a space or a line start — never by a word character.\n252\t#:\n253\t#: BOTH separators are `/+`, not `/` (pair-rail land round 1, P2):\n254\t#: `/Users//<owner>/repo` is the same directory to every filesystem and\n255\t#: every shell, and it read CLEAN (reproduced: `False` against `True` on\n256\t#: the single-slash form). A matcher that a doubled separator walks past is\n257\t#: a matcher with a one-keystroke bypass. MEASURED over every tracked file\n258\t#: before it was chosen: the widened pair and the v3 pair answer\n259\t#: IDENTICALLY on all 4238 hit lines — zero disagreements, in scope or out\n260\t#: — so this closes a hole and moves no row.\n261\t#:\n262\t#: The owner class is `\\w` (UNICODE), not `[A-Za-z0-9]` (pair-rail round 1,\n263\t#: P2). An ASCII-only class was wrong in BOTH directions: `/Users/<é>…` did\n264\t#: not match at all, and — worse — a name whose ASCII PREFIX is a placeholder\n265\t#: was TRUNCATED to it (`/Users/<user>/` read as owner `dev`, which the\n266\t#: placeholder set forgives), so a real owner was reported clean. Matching\n267\t#: the whole segment is what makes the placeholder comparison meaningful.\n268\t#: MEASURED before choosing it — the CENSUS above carries both\n269\t#: counts. The two pairs answer identically over every tracked file\n270\t#: but ONE line: the non-ASCII example this note spells out, which\n271\t#: is the very class the widening exists to catch. That line lives\n272\t#: under `.claude/scripts/`, out of this rule's scope, so the\n273\t#: correction moves no ROW on this tree.\n274\t#: The owner class is a DELIMITER class, not a character whitelist\n275\t#: (pair-rail round 1, P1). `[\\w.\\-]+` stopped at the first character it\n276\t#: did not know, and a segment read HALFWAY is worse than one not read at\n277\t#: all: `/Users/<user>/repo` was truncated to owner `dev`, which is on\n278\t#: the placeholder list, so a real owner was reported CLEAN. Round 1 of the\n279\t#: previous version fixed the ASCII half of exactly this and the class was\n280\t#: still a whitelist, so the shape came back for `+`, `$`, `~` and every\n281\t#: other legal filename character. Enumerating the legal ones is the thing\n282\t#: that keeps failing; the class now takes everything up to a character\n283\t#: that ENDS a path segment in prose.\n284\t#:\n285\t#: `<` and `>` are in the delimiter set, and that is what keeps the\n286\t#: documented placeholder style clean without a special case: in\n287\t#: `/Users/<user>/x` the very first character after the literal is a\n288\t#: delimiter, so there is no match at all — not a match the placeholder\n289\t#: list has to forgive.\n290\t#:\n291\t#: MEASURED over every tracked file, BOTH matchers together, before it\n292\t#: was chosen: 20 lines change answer (4238 -> 4258), ALL of them out of\n293\t#: this rule's scope, and every one is the widening being RIGHT — a shell\n294\t#: `${USER_NAME}` expansion, regex sources spelling `/Users/<user>]...`,\n295\t#: and byte fixtures in this rule's own tests. ZERO in-scope\n296\t#: disagreements, so zero rows move. The cost is declared: a regex or a\n297\t#: shell variable written inside `docs/` now needs a row, which is the\n298\t#: recoverable failure this rule is built around.\n299\t#:\n300\t#: BOTH matchers are built from ONE delimiter set, below. Round 2 of the\n301\t#: rail found the reason that matters: the first cure widened the DIRECT\n302\t#: matcher and left the SLUG one a whitelist, so `-Users-<user><real>-repo`\n303\t#: still read as owner `dev` — the same defect, one regex over. Two\n304\t#: matchers of the same thing that can be edited apart WILL be edited\n305\t#: apart; the shared constant is what makes that impossible rather than\n306\t#: unlikely.\n307\t_PERSONAL_PATH_SEG_DELIMS = r\"\"\"/\\s\"'`,;:)\\]}<>|\"\"\"\n308\t#: The lookbehind excludes a WORD character, a dot and a tilde -- the\n309\t#: forms that make the slash part of something else (`x/Users/`,\n310\t#: `./Users/`, `~/Users/`). It used to exclude `-` as well, which made\n311\t#: a unified-diff DELETION line invisible: `-/Users/<real>/work` in a\n312\t#: frozen diff read clean while the `+` line beside it was a hit\n313\t#: (pair-rail round 4, P1). A hyphen does not attach a path to a\n314\t#: preceding word the way a letter does, and MEASURED over every\n315\t#: tracked file the removal moves ZERO lines.\n316\t#:\n317\t#: The ROOT TOKEN is CASE-FOLDED; nothing else is. On the default\n318\t#: case-insensitive macOS volume this rule was written for,\n319\t#: `/users/<owner>/x` names the same directory as `/Users/<owner>/x`,\n320\t#: and the exact-case matcher answered False for it -- a decision made\n321\t#: by SPELLING rather than by content, which is the failure this whole\n322\t#: rule exists to remove (pair-rail round 16, P2). `/USERS/`, `/Home/`\n323\t#: and `/HOME/` were the same hole.\n324\t#:\n325\t#: Written as explicit per-character classes rather than a pattern-wide\n326\t#: `re.IGNORECASE`. The honest reason is LOCALITY, not behaviour: a flag\n327\t#: would not change what is captured (the owner segment keeps its own\n328\t#: case either way) and the delimiter class holds no letters. The two\n329\t#: spellings are NOT equivalent, and saying they were was the second\n330\t#: overstatement in this note: `re.IGNORECASE` folds U+017F LATIN SMALL\n331\t#: LETTER LONG S onto `s` and the explicit `[Ss]` class does not\n332\t#: (measured: ignorecase True, explicit False -- text lane, round 23).\n333\t#: The explicit classes are therefore the NARROWER spelling as well as\n334\t#: the local one, and a letter entering either class would widen the\n335\t#: fold silently. The classes put the fold where a\n336\t#: reader can see its extent. (Stated wrongly here once, as a\n337\t#: behavioural claim, and caught by the round-17 text lane.)\n338\t#:\n339\t#: MEASURED before shipping. The comparison only means something on the\n340\t#: tree WITHOUT the cure: run over a tree that already folds, the\n341\t#: instrument compares the fold against itself and answers zero for\n342\t#: free. `measure-case-variant-root.py` therefore reads its matcher from\n343\t#: the tree it is pointed at, and the number quoted here is its run\n344\t#: against the PRE-cure derivation -- see the CENSUS block above and\n345\t#: EVIDENCE section J, which also carry the two different in-scope\n346\t#: counts (the census counts with git pathspec globs, the instrument\n347\t#: with this rule's own scope predicate) so neither reads as a\n348\t#: correction of the other. The finding: the fold moves ZERO lines.\n349\t#:\n350\t#: `/users/<user>`, `/Users/<user>/x` and `/users/<user>/x` stay clean because\n351\t#: `me` and `alice` are PLACEHOLDERS. That is why the estimate of five\n352\t#: false positives does not reproduce -- FOUR of those lines are the HTTP\n353\t#: route `/users/<user>` (three in one document, one in a frozen transcript)\n354\t#: and the fifth is a `HOME/CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR` example, which is not a\n355\t#: home path at all. The estimate was made against an implementation\n356\t#: that had lost the placeholder filter.\n357\t#:\n358\t#: The narrower form proposed first -- fold only when the owner segment\n359\t#: matches the repo private-term pattern -- was measured and REJECTED,\n360\t#: with the reason stated exactly. `build_pattern()` never matches\n361\t#: nothing: it always carries `_PLACEHOLDER_TERMS`. In an adopter, with\n362\t#: no `scripts/contamination-terms.txt`, it reduces TO those three\n363\t#: alternatives -- so the narrowed fold would fire only for an owner\n364\t#: segment spelled like one of the three `_PLACEHOLDER_TERMS` at the top\n365\t#: of this file, and never for a real handle. (Those three are NOT\n366\t#: written out here: this module must not match the pattern it builds,\n367\t#: and quoting them made it do exactly that -- caught by\n368\t#: `test_delivered_module_is_not_itself_contaminated`, which is the\n369\t#: PLAN-183 W2 A7 guard doing its job on this very edit.)\n370\t#: A cure that fires only where its author declared a\n371\t#: terms file is a fail-open by configuration: the class this file\n372\t#: closes, not one it adds.\n373\t#: The leading run is ONE literal `/` guarded by a fixed-width\n374\t#: lookbehind, not `/+`. `/+` after a lookbehind can restart at every\n375\t#: slash of a run, and each restart rescans the rest of it: `\"/\" * 16000`\n376\t#: followed by a home path took 1.833 s on the machine that measured it,\n377\t#: and 8 000 took 0.48 s -- quadratic, on a line a committed file can\n378\t#: carry (mechanism lane, round 21, A1; reproduced). The slash is\n379\t#: CONSUMED and there is ONE lookbehind, `(?<![\\w.~]/)`, which carries\n380\t#: the same start condition the old pattern did: it refuses the position\n381\t#: only when the character before that slash makes it part of something\n382\t#: else AND there is no second slash -- which is exactly what\n383\t#: `(?<![\\w.~])/+` said. (An earlier draft of this note described two\n384\t#: lookbehinds and quoted a `(?<=/)` that the final pattern does not\n385\t#: have, because the slash was made a literal so the mask could keep\n386\t#: replacing it -- text lane, round 23.) Verified as a DIFFERENTIAL over\n387\t#: every in-scope tracked file plus the boundary forms `/Users/<user>`,\n388\t#: `//Users/<user>`, `a/Users/x`, `a//Users/<user>`, `./Users/x`, `~/Users/x`:\n389\t#: zero disagreements. The match now begins at the LAST slash of the\n390\t#: run rather than the first, which is why the `/` is CONSUMED and not\n391\t#: asserted -- `_personal_path_mask_rel` replaces the match, and a mask\n392\t#: that dropped the slash would rewrite the path it masks.\n393\t_PERSONAL_PATH_HOME_RE = re.compile(\n394\t    r\"/(?<![\\w.~]/)(?:[Uu][Ss][Ee][Rr][Ss]|[Hh][Oo][Mm][Ee])/+([^\"\n395\t    + _PERSONAL_PATH_SEG_DELIMS + \"]+)\"\n396\t)\n397\t\n398\t#: The harness slug form (`/` replaced by `-`), e.g. a project state directory\n399\t#: named `-Users-<name>-<repo>`. `-` separates segments here, so it cannot be\n400\t#: part of one: the capture is lazy up to the next `-`. `-+` for the same\n401\t#: reason the two above are `/+`: the slug of `/Users//<owner>` is\n402\t#: `-Users--<owner>`, and it read clean. Same differential, same answer:\n403\t#: zero disagreements over every tracked file.\n404\t#:\n405\t#: The owner class is the SHARED delimiter set plus `-`, which is this\n406\t#: form's own separator. Built from the same constant as the direct\n407\t#: matcher (pair-rail round 2, P1): widening one and not the other left\n408\t#: `-Users-<user><real>-repo` reading as the placeholder `dev`.\n409\t#:\n410\t#: The TERMINATOR is that same class, and it took a second round to be\n411\t#: (mechanism lane, round 20). It used to be `(?:-|$)` -- a hyphen or the\n412\t#: end of the line -- so any ordinary punctuation after the owner hid the\n413\t#: whole slug: `~/.claude/projects/-Users-<owner>/memory/`,\n414\t#: `docs/-Users-<owner>/note.md` and a quoted `\"-Users-<owner>\"` all read\n415\t#: CLEAN while `-Users-<owner>-repo` was a hit. The class the capture\n416\t#: cannot CONTAIN and the class it may END at are the same class, which\n417\t#: is the only spelling of this that has no third case.\n418\t#:\n419\t#: MEASURED over 2 189 in-scope tracked files and 1 744 389 lines before\n420\t#: it shipped (`measure-slug-terminator.py`): the widening alone moved SIX\n421\t#: lines, all six the elided `-Users-<ellipsis>` in prose about the slug\n422\t#: families. That elision is what the placeholder list is FOR, so the\n423\t#: Unicode ellipsis joined it beside the ASCII spelling, and the pair\n424\t#: together move ZERO lines.\n425\t#: The root token folds here for the same reason and in the same way as\n426\t#: in the direct matcher above: a slug is derived from a path, so a\n427\t#: case-variant path yields a case-variant slug, and one matcher folding\n428\t#: while its twin did not is how `-Users-<user><real>-repo` got through\n429\t#: once already (round 2, P1). Both fold, from the same reasoning.\n430\t_PERSONAL_PATH_SLUG_RE = re.compile(\n431\t    r\"-[Uu][Ss][Ee][Rr][Ss]-+([^\" + _PERSONAL_PATH_SEG_DELIMS\n432\t    + r\"-]+?)(?=[-\" + _PERSONAL_PATH_SEG_DELIMS + r\"]|$)\"\n433\t)\n434\t\n435\t#: Home-directory owner segments that are NEUTRAL PLACEHOLDERS, not people.\n436\t#: Docs and tests are supposed to use these, so flagging them would punish the\n437\t#: correct behaviour and push authors toward worse examples. Every entry is a\n438\t#: generic word or a CI/system account; adding a real handle here would make\n439\t#: this list the contamination it guards against (the PLAN-183 W2 A7 shape).\n440\t#: `git ls-files -s` renders a symlink with this mode. It is the ONLY\n441\t#: authority this rule accepts on whether an entry is a link, because the\n442\t#: filesystem is not one: under `core.symlinks=false` git materialises a\n443\t#: mode-120000 entry as a REGULAR FILE whose content is the target string,\n444\t#: so `Path.is_symlink()` answers False for a link: the link's TARGET\n445\t#: STRING would be scanned as ordinary content on one checkout and as a\n446\t#: link on another (pair-rail round 1, P1). Asking git\n447\t#: also removes the check/use race: the classification and the read no\n448\t#: longer come from two separate filesystem answers.\n449\t_PERSONAL_PATH_LINK_MODE = \"120000\"\n450\t\n451\t#: The index modes this rule knows how to turn into bytes: a regular\n452\t#: file, an executable file, and a symlink (whose blob is its target\n453\t#: string). ANY OTHER mode -- today the 160000 gitlink, tomorrow\n454\t#: whatever git adds -- is refused by name rather than skipped: a\n455\t#: gitlink in scope opened as a directory, yielded no blob, and still\n456\t#: counted as a file the walk had seen, so the scan reported clean\n457\t#: without reading it (pair-rail round 3, P1). A mode this rule cannot\n458\t#: read is an unverified entry, not a clean one.\n459\t_PERSONAL_PATH_BLOB_MODES = frozenset({\"100644\", \"100755\", \"120000\"})\n460\t\n461\t#: How many ancestors the allowlist symlink walk will examine before it\n462\t#: gives up -- and giving up is a REFUSAL, never a fall-through.\n463\t_PERSONAL_PATH_ANCESTOR_CAP = 64\n464\t\n465\t#: `O_NOFOLLOW` where the platform has it. A regular-file read opens with\n466\t#: it, so 'this is not a link' is decided by the SAME syscall that hands\n467\t#: back the inode — not by an `is_symlink()` a moment earlier that\n468\t#: something could invalidate before the read (pair-rail round 1, P1).\n469\t_PERSONAL_PATH_O_NOFOLLOW = getattr(os, \"O_NOFOLLOW\", 0)\n470\t\n471\t#: `open(O_RDONLY)` on a FIFO BLOCKS until a writer connects, so the\n472\t#: `fstat` that classifies the inode is never reached and the scan hangs\n473\t#: until an outer CI timeout instead of returning a verdict (pair-rail\n474\t#: round 3, P2; measured, still blocked at 25 s). `O_NONBLOCK` makes the\n475\t#: open return immediately for a non-regular inode, which is then\n476\t#: rejected by the fstat that already existed. It is a no-op for the\n477\t#: regular files this rule actually reads.\n478\t_PERSONAL_PATH_O_NONBLOCK = getattr(os, \"O_NONBLOCK\", 0)\n479\t\n480\t#: Compared lowercased.\n481\t_PERSONAL_PATH_PLACEHOLDERS = frozenset({\n482\t    \"...\",        # an elided segment, `/Users/<user>/project`\n483\t    \"\\u2026\",     # the SAME elision as one character, `-Users-<user>-repo`\n484\t    \"a\", \"b\", \"x\", \"y\", \"u\",\n485\t    \"adopter\", \"alice\", \"bob\", \"carol\",\n486\t    \"ci\", \"runner\", \"ubuntu\", \"root\",       # CI / container accounts\n487\t    \"dev\", \"devuser\", \"example\", \"home\", \"me\", \"someone\", \"tmp\",\n488\t    \"test\", \"testuser\", \"user\", \"username\", \"you\", \"youruser\",\n489\t})\n490\t\n491\t#: The tracked allowlist, NEXT TO this file, under `.claude/scripts/`.\n492\t#:\n493\t#: IT DOES NOT YET TRAVEL WITH A FRESH INSTALL, and saying that it did was\n494\t#: a claim about code that is not there (pair-rail round 2, P2):\n495\t#: `scripts/install.sh` copies `*.sh`, `*.py` and `*.yaml` out of this\n496\t#: directory and nothing copies a `.txt`. An adopter therefore gets the\n497\t#: rule and no allowlist, which the rule reads as EMPTY — fail-closed, so\n498\t#: their own findings are reported rather than silently waived, but it is\n499\t#: not the delivery this line used to promise. `install.sh` is CANONICAL,\n500\t#: so the fix is a signed ceremony (`scripts-fu`), not this free pack.\n501\t#: It must be TRACKED to waive anything: the gate confirms that with\n502\t#: git before it parses a row, and an untracked copy reads as empty.\n503\t_PERSONAL_PATH_ALLOWLIST_REL = (\n504\t    \".claude/scripts/contamination-personal-path-allowlist.txt\"\n505\t)\n506\t\n507\t#: An explicitly EMPTY value for a path option. `argparse` distinguishes\n508\t#: omission (`default=None`) from `--root \"\"`, and the module then read\n509\t#: both by truthiness: the empty string silently selected the DEFAULT, so\n510\t#: the gate scanned the checkout this file lives in and returned green\n511\t#: while the operator believed it had scanned somewhere else (pair-rail\n512\t#: round 4, P1). An empty path is not a path; it is a usage error.\n513\t_PERSONAL_PATH_EMPTY_OPTION = (\n514\t    \"%s was given an EMPTY value; an empty path is not a path, and \"\n515\t    \"treating it as \\\"not given\\\" would silently scan the default \"\n516\t    \"instead\"\n517\t)\n518\t\n519\t#: A reason an emitted row still carries. Refused by the parser so a row\n520\t#: pasted from `--emit-personal-path-rows` cannot be committed unreasoned.\n521\t_PERSONAL_PATH_UNREASONED = \"REPLACE-WITH-REASON\"\n522\t\n523\t#: Cap on what the CLI prints per section — the shipped allowlist waives\n524\t#: hundreds of frozen artifacts, and a wall of them hides the one that matters.\n525\t_PERSONAL_PATH_PRINT_CAP = 20\n526\t\n527\t#: EVERY alternative of BOTH regexes above requires one of these literals\n528\t#: UP TO THE CASE OF THE ROOT TOKEN, so a line holding none of them -- in\n529\t#: any case spelling -- cannot match either. That is why the fast path is\n530\t#: `_PERSONAL_PATH_LITERAL_RE` below and no longer `str.__contains__`:\n531\t#: since v4.9 the matchers fold the root, and an exact-case containment\n532\t#: test would reject `/users/<owner>/x` before either matcher ran -- the\n533\t#: fast path would have become a filter of its own, which is exactly what\n534\t#: this note promised it never would (round 17, text lane: the paragraph\n535\t#: here described code that had already been replaced). It remains a\n536\t#: SEMANTIC EQUIVALENCE, not a performance claim — the framework makes no\n537\t#: speed claim (CLAUDE.md §1), and pair-rail round 1 (P2) removed the\n538\t#: measured one that stood here. A differential test drives the predicate\n539\t#: and a regex-only reference over the same corpus and demands the same\n540\t#: answer.\n541\t_PERSONAL_PATH_LITERALS = (\"/Users/<user>", \"/home/<user>", \"-Users-<user>")\n542\t\n543\t#: The fast path asks the SAME question as the matchers, so it folds the\n544\t#: root token the same way -- and it is DERIVED from the tuple above\n545\t#: rather than written out a second time. A second spelling of the same\n546\t#: authority that silently omits the fold is the false-green shape this\n547\t#: file removes wherever it finds it: before this, the fast path rejected\n548\t#: `/users/<owner>/x` by exact case and the folded matchers below were\n549\t#: never reached (pair-rail round 16, P2).\n550\t_PERSONAL_PATH_LITERAL_RE = re.compile(\"|\".join(\n551\t    \"\".join(\n552\t        \"[%s%s]\" % (char.upper(), char.lower()) if char.isalpha()\n553\t        else re.escape(char)\n554\t        for char in literal\n555\t    )\n556\t    for literal in _PERSONAL_PATH_LITERALS\n557\t))\n558\t\n559\t#: Seconds a SINGLE in-scope file may spend in the line matcher before\n560\t#: the scan refuses it. The matcher is linear per line since the cure\n561\t#: above, so this is not the fix for that defect and does not claim to\n562\t#: be: it is the boundary for the general case, an arbitrary tracked file\n563\t#: fed to a regex engine. Fail-CLOSED, like every other unreadable INPUT\n564\t#: here (CLAUDE.md section 4): the file is REFUSED, rc 2, never reported\n565\t#: clean. Generous on purpose -- the largest in-scope file in this\n566\t#: repository scans in well under a second -- because a deadline that\n567\t#: fires on a slow runner turns a gate into a coin toss.\n568\t_PERSONAL_PATH_FILE_BUDGET_S = 30.0\n569\t\n570\t#: What `bytes.decode(errors=\"replace\")` leaves where a byte was not valid\n571\t#: UTF-8. Its presence makes the line's FIRST reading untrustworthy — see\n572\t#: `line_is_personal_path_hit`.\n573\t#:\n574\t#: DECLARED FALSE POSITIVE (pair-rail round 4, P2): U+FFFD is itself a\n575\t#: valid character, so a name or a line that CONTAINS one deliberately is\n576\t#: read here as a decoding failure. By the time this rule sees a name,\n577\t#: `FileWalker` has already decoded it with `errors=\"replace\"`, so the\n578\t#: two cases are no longer distinguishable at this layer. The direction\n579\t#: is the safe one -- the gate REFUSES rather than waives, and the\n580\t#: remedy it prints (rename the file) is correct for both -- and the fix\n581\t#: means carrying raw bytes through the walker, which is a change to the\n582\t#: older rule's contract.\n583\t_PERSONAL_PATH_REPLACEMENT = \"�\"\n584\t\n585\t#: Every mark a decoder can leave where it could not read a byte: U+FFFD\n586\t#: from `errors=\"replace\"`, and the lone surrogates U+DC80-U+DCFF from\n587\t#: `errors=\"surrogateescape\"` — which is what the OS hands back for a\n588\t#: symlink target and what the symlink branch used to feed this matcher\n589\t#: RAW (pair-rail land round 1, P1: the surrogate is not U+FFFD, so the\n590\t#: second reading never fired and `/Users/<user><0xFF>zzfake/` read as the\n591\t#: placeholder `dev`). One regex, so a new marker is added in one place.\n592\t_PERSONAL_PATH_UNDECODABLE_RE = re.compile(\"[\\ufffd\\udc80-\\udcff]\")\n593\t\n594\t\n595\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50141584-ed08-437c-bb91-f56d610ea253","uuid":"22d66bc0-2c84-4008-986d-9b6e0b9d6133","timestamp":"2026-11-13T18:36:35.805Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_contamination/personal_constants.py","content":"\"\"\"Rule 2 constants and policy rationale for the contamination CLI.\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport errno\nimport os\nimport re\nimport stat\nimport time\n\n# RULE 2 — `personal-path`: absolute home paths under docs/ and .claude/plans/\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# The TERM rule above exempts those two trees almost entirely: `.claude/plans/*`\n# is a WHOLESALE glob (fnmatch's `*` crosses `/`) and most of `docs/` sits on\n# the exact allowlist. Both exemptions were written for PROSE that legitimately\n# names adopter context; neither was ever a decision about absolute HOME paths.\n# This repository is PUBLIC, and the plan tree is where frozen rail transcripts\n# and ceremony logs land — which is exactly where a `/Users/<name>/` leaks.\n# PLAN-186 §Riscos recorded the follow-up (Codex P1, S339); this rule is it.\n#\n# WHAT IT FLAGS: an absolute home directory whose owner segment is a REAL name\n# — `/Users/<name>/...`, `/home/<name>/...`, and the harness slug form\n# `-Users-<name>-...` that a project state directory carries. The documented\n# placeholder style (`/Users/<user>/`, `/Users/<user>/`, `/home/<user>/`) is\n# not a hit: `<` is not a path character, and the generic owner segments are\n# listed below.\n#\n# NOTHING IS EXEMPT BY NAME OR BY SUFFIX. Every in-scope tracked path is\n# read and matched the SAME WAY -- no basename, no extension and no\n# directory decides that a file is not looked at. Three versions of this\n# pack shipped a name-shaped skip and the pair-rail found each of them:\n# `*.asc` exempt by suffix (v2, land round 1), `*-approved.md` exempt by\n# basename (v2, round cure-2), and `OWNER-*.sh` exempt by basename BEFORE\n# any content was read (v3, land round 1 -- the finding that dropped the\n# pack). Each time the bypass was one rename: a tracked symlink\n# `docs/OWNER-leak.sh -> /Users/<owner>/repo` walked through the gate that\n# `docs/plain-leak.md` with the SAME target failed. The class is closed by\n# REMOVAL, not by enumerating the safe names -- which is the only closure\n# this repository has ever made stick (CLAUDE.md, S336 lesson).\n#\n# WAIVERS therefore act on a hit that has ALREADY been found, and come\n# from exactly ONE place:\n#   * the tracked allowlist file, ONE `<path> | sha256:<64 hex> | <reason>`\n#     row per file. A row is a human decision with the decision written\n#     down, and BOTH halves are checked: the reason must contain a\n#     character that is actually shown and none that is a control or a\n#     format character, so a reason of zero-width spaces -- which\n#     satisfied the grammar and waived a real leak (round 19) -- is\n#     MALFORMED. There is no glob, no directory prefix and no generated\n#     baseline, because every shape that waives a file nobody named is the\n#     shape this rule exists to remove. THE ROW IS PINNED TO CONTENT: it\n#     waives the file only while the bytes the scan read hash to the\n#     recorded digest, so a row cannot waive a file FOREVER (pair-rail,\n#     land round 1, P2) -- one added byte and the waiver lapses, the gate\n#     goes red, and a human re-reads the file and re-records the digest.\n#     TRACKEDNESS IS VERIFIED, not assumed: an allowlist git cannot\n#     confirm is read as EMPTY, so a `touch`ed copy waives nothing\n#     (`_personal_path_allowlist_is_tracked`).\n#\n# THERE IS NO SECOND WAIVER, AND NO RULE WAIVER AT ALL. A signed\n# sentinel (`approved.md` / `*-approved.md`) used to be forgiven by a\n# RULE that asked four questions of content -- a sibling `.asc`, an\n# armoured signature body over a size floor, an `Approved-By`\n# fingerprint a tracked row granted, and not-a-symlink. Every one of\n# those answers is TRANSPLANTABLE by anyone who can write a file: the\n# sidecar is not bound to the document, and the Owner's fingerprint is\n# published. So `docs/leak-approved.md` with a copied `.asc` was waived\n# while the same bytes under another name were a finding -- exemption\n# by NAME in a content-shaped costume, which is the one class this rule\n# exists to remove. It waived ZERO hits on this tree (CENSUS below), so\n# deleting it moved no row and closed the surface outright.\n#\n# A signature this gate can actually VERIFY would be a second waiver\n# earned by content; that needs `gpg` and a keyring, and it is\n# `.claude/plans/PLAN-186-FOLLOWUP-sentinel-gpg-verification.md`. Until\n# it lands, a frozen signed artifact carrying a home path is waived the\n# same way everything else is: by a row pinned to its digest -- and a\n# signature freezes that digest, so the row cannot go stale behind\n# anyone's back.\n#\n# The rule NEVER prints a matched LINE — its findings land in a PUBLIC CI log,\n# and a gate that quotes the leak it found is a second copy of it. It prints\n# `file:line`, which is what a human needs to go and look.\n#\n# THAT RULE APPLIES TO THE GATE'S OWN OUTPUT, at one choke point. Everything\n# this rule prints that it did not compose itself — a waiver REASON, an\n# allowlist path outside the repo, an ignored CLI token, the REASON an\n# OSError carries — goes through `_personal_path_safe`, which runs the\n# rule's own predicate over the string and replaces it wholesale when it\n# names a home directory. Two of those fatal prints were interpolating\n# `_oserror_reason(exc)` RAW until the text lane of round 20 injected an\n# error whose diagnostic carried a home path and watched it print at rc 2;\n# an exhaustive-sounding sentence beside four safe call sites is how the\n# fifth stays unnoticed. There is ONE deliberate exception and it is not a\n# print: the row offered by `--emit-personal-path-rows`, which the parser\n# has to read back, is built from `hit.rel` under two explicit refusals --\n# see `_personal_path_safe`. Four separate\n# print sites were found leaking by the pair-rail (round cure-1, P2 x3); they\n# were four instances of one shape, so the cure is one predicate at the\n# boundary, not four redactions. A contaminated waiver reason is ALSO refused\n# at parse time, so it never reaches a printer at all.\n#\n# A tracked SYMLINK is scanned as what git stores: the TARGET STRING. Not\n# doing so was a false negative with a one-line trigger (rail round cure-1,\n# P1) — `is_file()` follows the link, so a dangling link read as \"absent\" and\n# a resolvable one had someone else's file scanned in its place, while the\n# `/Users/<name>/…` in the committed link payload was never looked at.\n#\n# FAIL-CLOSED ON INPUT (CLAUDE.md §4): an allowlist that exists and cannot be\n# read or DECODED (a single non-UTF-8 byte used to raise through the CLI as a\n# traceback carrying the absolute checkout path — rail round cure-1, P2), and\n# an in-scope file that exists and cannot be read, are both fatal\n# (rc 2) — an unverified scan is not a clean one. That fatal names the file the\n# way every other line of this rule does — repo-relative, NEVER absolute:\n# `str(OSError)` embeds `filename`, and on an operator machine that filename is\n# the very `/Users/<name>/...` this rule exists to keep out of a public log\n# (`_oserror_reason` below is what strips it; measured, cured,\n# controlled). A file that is TRACKED but\n# ABSENT from the worktree is skipped: absence is not unreadable input. Bytes\n# that are not valid UTF-8 are REPLACED, never skipped, so \"the guard could not\n# parse it\" cannot read as \"the guard found nothing\".\n#\n# DECLARED BLIND SPOTS (a rule that names what it cannot see):\n#   * a path split across two lines, or assembled from fragments;\n#   * the Windows form, and the `-home-<name>-` slug form (the latter would\n#     fire on ordinary hyphenated prose such as `-home-page-`);\n#   * a HYPHENATED owner in the SLUG form only: `-Users-<user>-jane-repo` reads\n#     as owner `dev`, a placeholder, and is missed (pair-rail round cure-2,\n#     P2 — DECLARED, not cured). Reading further would mean treating\n#     `-Users-<user>-work-repo` as owner `runner-work`, which is not on the\n#     placeholder list, so the fail-closed reading fires on the documented\n#     placeholder style the rule exists to protect. The direct form\n#     `/Users/<user>/` IS caught (that regex accepts `-`), and a document\n#     carrying the slug almost always carries the direct form too;\n#   * a relative `Users/<name>/...` with no leading slash;\n#   * a personal path glued straight onto a preceding word character\n#     (`x/Users/<name>`) — the price of not firing on every `.../home/<page>`\n#     URL route, a shape docs legitimately carry;\n#   * TWO undecodable bytes in one line: the second reading removes every\n#     U+FFFD at once, so `/Us<FF>ers/<FF>name` is covered, but a byte that\n#     decodes to a VALID character (rather than to U+FFFD) is not modelled at\n#     all — this rule is a leak detector, not an adversary-proof filter;\n#   * THE INDEX IS ENUMERATED AND THE WORKTREE IS READ. `git ls-files`\n#     names the entries; the bytes come from the filesystem. Stage a\n#     leaking blob and then overwrite or delete it in the worktree and\n#     the scan sees the clean copy, while `git commit` lands the staged\n#     one -- and a row pins the WORKTREE bytes, not necessarily the bytes\n#     being landed (pair-rail round 1, P1, both lanes). DECLARED, not\n#     cured: closing it means reading blobs through `git cat-file`\n#     instead of the filesystem, which changes what every digest in the\n#     shipped allowlist means. It is also the pre-existing behaviour of\n#     RULE 1 and of `FileWalker`, and in CI -- where this gate runs -- the\n#     index and the worktree are the same tree by construction;\n#   * the owner class is a DELIMITER class, so an owner segment that\n#     CONTAINS one of the delimiters (`<`, `>`, a quote, a comma, a\n#     closing bracket) is still read short. That set is exactly what\n#     keeps `/Users/<user>/` clean without a special case, and the trade\n#     is deliberate: the alternative fires on the documented placeholder\n#     style this rule exists to encourage;\n#   * `/./Users/<name>/...` is missed -- the lookbehind rejects a leading\n#     `.`, which is what keeps the rule off `../home/x` and\n#     `example.com/home/index`. Same family as the relative form above;\n#   * a row waives the WHOLE file AT ONE DIGEST: two leaks added in the\n#     same edit are waived together by the row that records that edit's\n#     sha256. What a row can no longer do is waive the NEXT edit -- the\n#     digest is the thing a reviewer re-approves;\n#   * a FROZEN artifact is waived by a row like anything else. There is\n#     no rule waiver to earn, so the operator cost of a signed sentinel\n#     that really does carry a home path is one row -- and because a\n#     signature freezes the bytes, that row's digest cannot lapse\n#     without the signature lapsing first;\n#   * a symlink's TARGET is scanned as text, so a link to a file whose\n#     CONTENT carries a personal path is judged by the link, not by that\n#     file — the file itself is scanned on its own if it is tracked and in\n#     scope, and out of scope it was never this rule's subject;\n#   * TRACKED is not COMMITTED. The allowlist's trackedness is\n#     verified, so a file git never heard of waives nothing — but an\n#     UNCOMMITTED edit to a tracked allowlist still grants. That edit\n#     is a `git diff`, which is the reviewable artifact the claim was\n#     always about, and refusing it would make \"add the row, run the\n#     gate, commit it in the same change\" impossible in that order.\n#\n# CENSUS — the table most \"MEASURED\" notes below are derived from. Two are\n# not, and say so where they stand: the case-variant fold and the NUL\n# refusal each name their own instrument, because each counts a different\n# population than this table does. A note that cites this table when it\n# measured something else would be the drift this block exists to stop.\n#\n# It is the SOURCE, not the only occurrence: a note repeats a figure\n# from it inline (the 242 waived files) because the\n# sentence is unreadable without it. Each says CENSUS beside the number\n# so a reader knows where it came from, and a claim of exclusivity that\n# the file itself contradicts is worse than none (pair-rail round 3, P3).\n#\n# Every \"MEASURED\" note below cites this table instead of carrying a figure\n# of its own. Three of them had already drifted by the time a cross-vendor\n# review read the module (S344): two docstrings were a generation behind the\n# disk, while sibling comments in this same file carried the current ones. A\n# number written in six places is six chances to be wrong; written once,\n# with the command beside it, it is one thing to re-run.\n#\n# Measured 2026-09-04 (S344) at 056e11c, on the tree with this rule applied\n# and staged. From the repository root:\n#\n#   git ls-files -z | tr '\\0' '\\n' | grep -c .                      -> 5222\n#   git ls-files 'docs/*' '.claude/plans/*' | wc -l                 -> 2171\n#   git ls-files 'docs/*' '.claude/plans/*' \\\n#     | grep -cE '(^|/)(approved|.*-approved)\\.md$'                 ->   75\n#   ... the same list, counting those with `test -f \"$f.asc\"`       ->   75\n#   git ls-files 'docs/*' '.claude/plans/*' | grep -c '\\.asc$'      ->   82\n#\n# The rest is this module's own predicates driven over `git ls-files`, plus\n# one `python3 .claude/scripts/check_contamination.py` for the verdict line:\n#\n#   * in-scope `.asc`: 0 are symlinks, 0 carry a hit line, and every one\n#     decodes to the same 147-byte signature body;\n#   * in-scope sentinels: 75, and NONE of them carries a hit. That is the\n#     number that made the DELETION of the rule waiver free: 34 of them\n#     passed the four checks the deleted rule asked, and not one of those\n#     34 had anything to be forgiven. A waiver that forgives nothing is\n#     an attack surface with no user;\n#   * in-scope files carrying a hit: 242 — each one waived by a row whose\n#     digest matches, with 0 unwaived, 0 digest mismatches, 0 stale and\n#     0 malformed rows;\n#   * what the REMOVED name exemptions were hiding, measured on this tree\n#     before they were removed: of 54 in-scope `OWNER-*.sh` scripts, 0\n#     carry a hit (they name the checkout through `$HOME` and `$CEO_REPO`,\n#     never literally); of 75 in-scope sentinels, 0 carry a hit; 0 in-scope\n#     paths are symlinks; 0 in-scope regular files carry a binary suffix.\n#     EVERY exemption this rule ever had waived exactly nothing — so the\n#     uniform scan and the deleted `_SKIP_SUFFIXES` branch move ZERO rows,\n#     and the waived count is the same 242 before and after;\n#   * in-scope paths hitting on their own NAME: 0. Tracked paths that are\n#     undecodable in scope: 0;\n#   * hit lines over every tracked file: 4238 under the widened `\\w` pair\n#     and 4237 under the ASCII one. The ONE line they disagree on is the\n#     non-ASCII example spelled out in the `_PERSONAL_PATH_HOME_RE` note\n#     below — a line under `.claude/scripts/`, which this rule never scans,\n#     so the widening moves no ROW.\n#\n# These figures AGE with the tree; they are dated and pinned to a base so a\n# reader can tell staleness from drift. Re-run the commands before quoting\n# them, and change them HERE — not in the prose that cites them.\n\n#: Only these two trees. The rule deliberately does NOT widen the walk — the\n#: term rule already sees everything else.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_SCOPE = (\".claude/plans/\", \"docs/\")\n\n#: `/Users/<seg>` and `/home/<seg>`. The lookbehind keeps the rule off URL\n#: routes and nested directories (`example.com/home/index`, `/srv/home/x`):\n#: a home path is written from a path root, so it is preceded by `/`, a quote,\n#: a space or a line start — never by a word character.\n#:\n#: BOTH separators are `/+`, not `/` (pair-rail land round 1, P2):\n#: `/Users//<owner>/repo` is the same directory to every filesystem and\n#: every shell, and it read CLEAN (reproduced: `False` against `True` on\n#: the single-slash form). A matcher that a doubled separator walks past is\n#: a matcher with a one-keystroke bypass. MEASURED over every tracked file\n#: before it was chosen: the widened pair and the v3 pair answer\n#: IDENTICALLY on all 4238 hit lines — zero disagreements, in scope or out\n#: — so this closes a hole and moves no row.\n#:\n#: The owner class is `\\w` (UNICODE), not `[A-Za-z0-9]` (pair-rail round 1,\n#: P2). An ASCII-only class was wrong in BOTH directions: `/Users/<é>…` did\n#: not match at all, and — worse — a name whose ASCII PREFIX is a placeholder\n#: was TRUNCATED to it (`/Users/<user>/` read as owner `dev`, which the\n#: placeholder set forgives), so a real owner was reported clean. Matching\n#: the whole segment is what makes the placeholder comparison meaningful.\n#: MEASURED before choosing it — the CENSUS above carries both\n#: counts. The two pairs answer identically over every tracked file\n#: but ONE line: the non-ASCII example this note spells out, which\n#: is the very class the widening exists to catch. That line lives\n#: under `.claude/scripts/`, out of this rule's scope, so the\n#: correction moves no ROW on this tree.\n#: The owner class is a DELIMITER class, not a character whitelist\n#: (pair-rail round 1, P1). `[\\w.\\-]+` stopped at the first character it\n#: did not know, and a segment read HALFWAY is worse than one not read at\n#: all: `/Users/<user>/repo` was truncated to owner `dev`, which is on\n#: the placeholder list, so a real owner was reported CLEAN. Round 1 of the\n#: previous version fixed the ASCII half of exactly this and the class was\n#: still a whitelist, so the shape came back for `+`, `$`, `~` and every\n#: other legal filename character. Enumerating the legal ones is the thing\n#: that keeps failing; the class now takes everything up to a character\n#: that ENDS a path segment in prose.\n#:\n#: `<` and `>` are in the delimiter set, and that is what keeps the\n#: documented placeholder style clean without a special case: in\n#: `/Users/<user>/x` the very first character after the literal is a\n#: delimiter, so there is no match at all — not a match the placeholder\n#: list has to forgive.\n#:\n#: MEASURED over every tracked file, BOTH matchers together, before it\n#: was chosen: 20 lines change answer (4238 -> 4258), ALL of them out of\n#: this rule's scope, and every one is the widening being RIGHT — a shell\n#: `${USER_NAME}` expansion, regex sources spelling `/Users/<user>]...`,\n#: and byte fixtures in this rule's own tests. ZERO in-scope\n#: disagreements, so zero rows move. The cost is declared: a regex or a\n#: shell variable written inside `docs/` now needs a row, which is the\n#: recoverable failure this rule is built around.\n#:\n#: BOTH matchers are built from ONE delimiter set, below. Round 2 of the\n#: rail found the reason that matters: the first cure widened the DIRECT\n#: matcher and left the SLUG one a whitelist, so `-Users-<user><real>-repo`\n#: still read as owner `dev` — the same defect, one regex over. Two\n#: matchers of the same thing that can be edited apart WILL be edited\n#: apart; the shared constant is what makes that impossible rather than\n#: unlikely.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_SEG_DELIMS = r\"\"\"/\\s\"'`,;:)\\]}<>|\"\"\"\n#: The lookbehind excludes a WORD character, a dot and a tilde -- the\n#: forms that make the slash part of something else (`x/Users/`,\n#: `./Users/`, `~/Users/`). It used to exclude `-` as well, which made\n#: a unified-diff DELETION line invisible: `-/Users/<real>/work` in a\n#: frozen diff read clean while the `+` line beside it was a hit\n#: (pair-rail round 4, P1). A hyphen does not attach a path to a\n#: preceding word the way a letter does, and MEASURED over every\n#: tracked file the removal moves ZERO lines.\n#:\n#: The ROOT TOKEN is CASE-FOLDED; nothing else is. On the default\n#: case-insensitive macOS volume this rule was written for,\n#: `/users/<owner>/x` names the same directory as `/Users/<owner>/x`,\n#: and the exact-case matcher answered False for it -- a decision made\n#: by SPELLING rather than by content, which is the failure this whole\n#: rule exists to remove (pair-rail round 16, P2). `/USERS/`, `/Home/`\n#: and `/HOME/` were the same hole.\n#:\n#: Written as explicit per-character classes rather than a pattern-wide\n#: `re.IGNORECASE`. The honest reason is LOCALITY, not behaviour: a flag\n#: would not change what is captured (the owner segment keeps its own\n#: case either way) and the delimiter class holds no letters. The two\n#: spellings are NOT equivalent, and saying they were was the second\n#: overstatement in this note: `re.IGNORECASE` folds U+017F LATIN SMALL\n#: LETTER LONG S onto `s` and the explicit `[Ss]` class does not\n#: (measured: ignorecase True, explicit False -- text lane, round 23).\n#: The explicit classes are therefore the NARROWER spelling as well as\n#: the local one, and a letter entering either class would widen the\n#: fold silently. The classes put the fold where a\n#: reader can see its extent. (Stated wrongly here once, as a\n#: behavioural claim, and caught by the round-17 text lane.)\n#:\n#: MEASURED before shipping. The comparison only means something on the\n#: tree WITHOUT the cure: run over a tree that already folds, the\n#: instrument compares the fold against itself and answers zero for\n#: free. `measure-case-variant-root.py` therefore reads its matcher from\n#: the tree it is pointed at, and the number quoted here is its run\n#: against the PRE-cure derivation -- see the CENSUS block above and\n#: EVIDENCE section J, which also carry the two different in-scope\n#: counts (the census counts with git pathspec globs, the instrument\n#: with this rule's own scope predicate) so neither reads as a\n#: correction of the other. The finding: the fold moves ZERO lines.\n#:\n#: `/users/<user>`, `/Users/<user>/x` and `/users/<user>/x` stay clean because\n#: `me` and `alice` are PLACEHOLDERS. That is why the estimate of five\n#: false positives does not reproduce -- FOUR of those lines are the HTTP\n#: route `/users/<user>` (three in one document, one in a frozen transcript)\n#: and the fifth is a `HOME/CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR` example, which is not a\n#: home path at all. The estimate was made against an implementation\n#: that had lost the placeholder filter.\n#:\n#: The narrower form proposed first -- fold only when the owner segment\n#: matches the repo private-term pattern -- was measured and REJECTED,\n#: with the reason stated exactly. `build_pattern()` never matches\n#: nothing: it always carries `_PLACEHOLDER_TERMS`. In an adopter, with\n#: no `scripts/contamination-terms.txt`, it reduces TO those three\n#: alternatives -- so the narrowed fold would fire only for an owner\n#: segment spelled like one of the three `_PLACEHOLDER_TERMS` at the top\n#: of this file, and never for a real handle. (Those three are NOT\n#: written out here: this module must not match the pattern it builds,\n#: and quoting them made it do exactly that -- caught by\n#: `test_delivered_module_is_not_itself_contaminated`, which is the\n#: PLAN-183 W2 A7 guard doing its job on this very edit.)\n#: A cure that fires only where its author declared a\n#: terms file is a fail-open by configuration: the class this file\n#: closes, not one it adds.\n#: The leading run is ONE literal `/` guarded by a fixed-width\n#: lookbehind, not `/+`. `/+` after a lookbehind can restart at every\n#: slash of a run, and each restart rescans the rest of it: `\"/\" * 16000`\n#: followed by a home path took 1.833 s on the machine that measured it,\n#: and 8 000 took 0.48 s -- quadratic, on a line a committed file can\n#: carry (mechanism lane, round 21, A1; reproduced). The slash is\n#: CONSUMED and there is ONE lookbehind, `(?<![\\w.~]/)`, which carries\n#: the same start condition the old pattern did: it refuses the position\n#: only when the character before that slash makes it part of something\n#: else AND there is no second slash -- which is exactly what\n#: `(?<![\\w.~])/+` said. (An earlier draft of this note described two\n#: lookbehinds and quoted a `(?<=/)` that the final pattern does not\n#: have, because the slash was made a literal so the mask could keep\n#: replacing it -- text lane, round 23.) Verified as a DIFFERENTIAL over\n#: every in-scope tracked file plus the boundary forms `/Users/<user>`,\n#: `//Users/<user>`, `a/Users/x`, `a//Users/<user>`, `./Users/x`, `~/Users/x`:\n#: zero disagreements. The match now begins at the LAST slash of the\n#: run rather than the first, which is why the `/` is CONSUMED and not\n#: asserted -- `_personal_path_mask_rel` replaces the match, and a mask\n#: that dropped the slash would rewrite the path it masks.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_HOME_RE = re.compile(\n    r\"/(?<![\\w.~]/)(?:[Uu][Ss][Ee][Rr][Ss]|[Hh][Oo][Mm][Ee])/+([^\"\n    + _PERSONAL_PATH_SEG_DELIMS + \"]+)\"\n)\n\n#: The harness slug form (`/` replaced by `-`), e.g. a project state directory\n#: named `-Users-<name>-<repo>`. `-` separates segments here, so it cannot be\n#: part of one: the capture is lazy up to the next `-`. `-+` for the same\n#: reason the two above are `/+`: the slug of `/Users//<owner>` is\n#: `-Users--<owner>`, and it read clean. Same differential, same answer:\n#: zero disagreements over every tracked file.\n#:\n#: The owner class is the SHARED delimiter set plus `-`, which is this\n#: form's own separator. Built from the same constant as the direct\n#: matcher (pair-rail round 2, P1): widening one and not the other left\n#: `-Users-<user><real>-repo` reading as the placeholder `dev`.\n#:\n#: The TERMINATOR is that same class, and it took a second round to be\n#: (mechanism lane, round 20). It used to be `(?:-|$)` -- a hyphen or the\n#: end of the line -- so any ordinary punctuation after the owner hid the\n#: whole slug: `~/.claude/projects/-Users-<owner>/memory/`,\n#: `docs/-Users-<owner>/note.md` and a quoted `\"-Users-<owner>\"` all read\n#: CLEAN while `-Users-<owner>-repo` was a hit. The class the capture\n#: cannot CONTAIN and the class it may END at are the same class, which\n#: is the only spelling of this that has no third case.\n#:\n#: MEASURED over 2 189 in-scope tracked files and 1 744 389 lines before\n#: it shipped (`measure-slug-terminator.py`): the widening alone moved SIX\n#: lines, all six the elided `-Users-<ellipsis>` in prose about the slug\n#: families. That elision is what the placeholder list is FOR, so the\n#: Unicode ellipsis joined it beside the ASCII spelling, and the pair\n#: together move ZERO lines.\n#: The root token folds here for the same reason and in the same way as\n#: in the direct matcher above: a slug is derived from a path, so a\n#: case-variant path yields a case-variant slug, and one matcher folding\n#: while its twin did not is how `-Users-<user><real>-repo` got through\n#: once already (round 2, P1). Both fold, from the same reasoning.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_SLUG_RE = re.compile(\n    r\"-[Uu][Ss][Ee][Rr][Ss]-+([^\" + _PERSONAL_PATH_SEG_DELIMS\n    + r\"-]+?)(?=[-\" + _PERSONAL_PATH_SEG_DELIMS + r\"]|$)\"\n)\n\n#: Home-directory owner segments that are NEUTRAL PLACEHOLDERS, not people.\n#: Docs and tests are supposed to use these, so flagging them would punish the\n#: correct behaviour and push authors toward worse examples. Every entry is a\n#: generic word or a CI/system account; adding a real handle here would make\n#: this list the contamination it guards against (the PLAN-183 W2 A7 shape).\n#: `git ls-files -s` renders a symlink with this mode. It is the ONLY\n#: authority this rule accepts on whether an entry is a link, because the\n#: filesystem is not one: under `core.symlinks=false` git materialises a\n#: mode-120000 entry as a REGULAR FILE whose content is the target string,\n#: so `Path.is_symlink()` answers False for a link: the link's TARGET\n#: STRING would be scanned as ordinary content on one checkout and as a\n#: link on another (pair-rail round 1, P1). Asking git\n#: also removes the check/use race: the classification and the read no\n#: longer come from two separate filesystem answers.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_LINK_MODE = \"120000\"\n\n#: The index modes this rule knows how to turn into bytes: a regular\n#: file, an executable file, and a symlink (whose blob is its target\n#: string). ANY OTHER mode -- today the 160000 gitlink, tomorrow\n#: whatever git adds -- is refused by name rather than skipped: a\n#: gitlink in scope opened as a directory, yielded no blob, and still\n#: counted as a file the walk had seen, so the scan reported clean\n#: without reading it (pair-rail round 3, P1). A mode this rule cannot\n#: read is an unverified entry, not a clean one.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_BLOB_MODES = frozenset({\"100644\", \"100755\", \"120000\"})\n\n#: How many ancestors the allowlist symlink walk will examine before it\n#: gives up -- and giving up is a REFUSAL, never a fall-through.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_ANCESTOR_CAP = 64\n\n#: `O_NOFOLLOW` where the platform has it. A regular-file read opens with\n#: it, so 'this is not a link' is decided by the SAME syscall that hands\n#: back the inode — not by an `is_symlink()` a moment earlier that\n#: something could invalidate before the read (pair-rail round 1, P1).\n_PERSONAL_PATH_O_NOFOLLOW = getattr(os, \"O_NOFOLLOW\", 0)\n\n#: `open(O_RDONLY)` on a FIFO BLOCKS until a writer connects, so the\n#: `fstat` that classifies the inode is never reached and the scan hangs\n#: until an outer CI timeout instead of returning a verdict (pair-rail\n#: round 3, P2; measured, still blocked at 25 s). `O_NONBLOCK` makes the\n#: open return immediately for a non-regular inode, which is then\n#: rejected by the fstat that already existed. It is a no-op for the\n#: regular files this rule actually reads.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_O_NONBLOCK = getattr(os, \"O_NONBLOCK\", 0)\n\n#: Compared lowercased.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_PLACEHOLDERS = frozenset({\n    \"...\",        # an elided segment, `/Users/<user>/project`\n    \"\\u2026\",     # the SAME elision as one character, `-Users-<user>-repo`\n    \"a\", \"b\", \"x\", \"y\", \"u\",\n    \"adopter\", \"alice\", \"bob\", \"carol\",\n    \"ci\", \"runner\", \"ubuntu\", \"root\",       # CI / container accounts\n    \"dev\", \"devuser\", \"example\", \"home\", \"me\", \"someone\", \"tmp\",\n    \"test\", \"testuser\", \"user\", \"username\", \"you\", \"youruser\",\n})\n\n#: The tracked allowlist, NEXT TO this file, under `.claude/scripts/`.\n#:\n#: IT DOES NOT YET TRAVEL WITH A FRESH INSTALL, and saying that it did was\n#: a claim about code that is not there (pair-rail round 2, P2):\n#: `scripts/install.sh` copies `*.sh`, `*.py` and `*.yaml` out of this\n#: directory and nothing copies a `.txt`. An adopter therefore gets the\n#: rule and no allowlist, which the rule reads as EMPTY — fail-closed, so\n#: their own findings are reported rather than silently waived, but it is\n#: not the delivery this line used to promise. `install.sh` is CANONICAL,\n#: so the fix is a signed ceremony (`scripts-fu`), not this free pack.\n#: It must be TRACKED to waive anything: the gate confirms that with\n#: git before it parses a row, and an untracked copy reads as empty.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_ALLOWLIST_REL = (\n    \".claude/scripts/contamination-personal-path-allowlist.txt\"\n)\n\n#: An explicitly EMPTY value for a path option. `argparse` distinguishes\n#: omission (`default=None`) from `--root \"\"`, and the module then read\n#: both by truthiness: the empty string silently selected the DEFAULT, so\n#: the gate scanned the checkout this file lives in and returned green\n#: while the operator believed it had scanned somewhere else (pair-rail\n#: round 4, P1). An empty path is not a path; it is a usage error.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_EMPTY_OPTION = (\n    \"%s was given an EMPTY value; an empty path is not a path, and \"\n    \"treating it as \\\"not given\\\" would silently scan the default \"\n    \"instead\"\n)\n\n#: A reason an emitted row still carries. Refused by the parser so a row\n#: pasted from `--emit-personal-path-rows` cannot be committed unreasoned.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_UNREASONED = \"REPLACE-WITH-REASON\"\n\n#: Cap on what the CLI prints per section — the shipped allowlist waives\n#: hundreds of frozen artifacts, and a wall of them hides the one that matters.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_PRINT_CAP = 20\n\n#: EVERY alternative of BOTH regexes above requires one of these literals\n#: UP TO THE CASE OF THE ROOT TOKEN, so a line holding none of them -- in\n#: any case spelling -- cannot match either. That is why the fast path is\n#: `_PERSONAL_PATH_LITERAL_RE` below and no longer `str.__contains__`:\n#: since v4.9 the matchers fold the root, and an exact-case containment\n#: test would reject `/users/<owner>/x` before either matcher ran -- the\n#: fast path would have become a filter of its own, which is exactly what\n#: this note promised it never would (round 17, text lane: the paragraph\n#: here described code that had already been replaced). It remains a\n#: SEMANTIC EQUIVALENCE, not a performance claim — the framework makes no\n#: speed claim (CLAUDE.md §1), and pair-rail round 1 (P2) removed the\n#: measured one that stood here. A differential test drives the predicate\n#: and a regex-only reference over the same corpus and demands the same\n#: answer.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_LITERALS = (\"/Users/<user>", \"/home/<user>", \"-Users-<user>")\n\n#: The fast path asks the SAME question as the matchers, so it folds the\n#: root token the same way -- and it is DERIVED from the tuple above\n#: rather than written out a second time. A second spelling of the same\n#: authority that silently omits the fold is the false-green shape this\n#: file removes wherever it finds it: before this, the fast path rejected\n#: `/users/<owner>/x` by exact case and the folded matchers below were\n#: never reached (pair-rail round 16, P2).\n_PERSONAL_PATH_LITERAL_RE = re.compile(\"|\".join(\n    \"\".join(\n        \"[%s%s]\" % (char.upper(), char.lower()) if char.isalpha()\n        else re.escape(char)\n        for char in literal\n    )\n    for literal in _PERSONAL_PATH_LITERALS\n))\n\n#: Seconds a SINGLE in-scope file may spend in the line matcher before\n#: the scan refuses it. The matcher is linear per line since the cure\n#: above, so this is not the fix for that defect and does not claim to\n#: be: it is the boundary for the general case, an arbitrary tracked file\n#: fed to a regex engine. Fail-CLOSED, like every other unreadable INPUT\n#: here (CLAUDE.md section 4): the file is REFUSED, rc 2, never reported\n#: clean. Generous on purpose -- the largest in-scope file in this\n#: repository scans in well under a second -- because a deadline that\n#: fires on a slow runner turns a gate into a coin toss.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_FILE_BUDGET_S = 30.0\n\n#: What `bytes.decode(errors=\"replace\")` leaves where a byte was not valid\n#: UTF-8. Its presence makes the line's FIRST reading untrustworthy — see\n#: `line_is_personal_path_hit`.\n#:\n#: DECLARED FALSE POSITIVE (pair-rail round 4, P2): U+FFFD is itself a\n#: valid character, so a name or a line that CONTAINS one deliberately is\n#: read here as a decoding failure. By the time this rule sees a name,\n#: `FileWalker` has already decoded it with `errors=\"replace\"`, so the\n#: two cases are no longer distinguishable at this layer. The direction\n#: is the safe one -- the gate REFUSES rather than waives, and the\n#: remedy it prints (rename the file) is correct for both -- and the fix\n#: means carrying raw bytes through the walker, which is a change to the\n#: older rule's contract.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_REPLACEMENT = \"�\"\n\n#: Every mark a decoder can leave where it could not read a byte: U+FFFD\n#: from `errors=\"replace\"`, and the lone surrogates U+DC80-U+DCFF from\n#: `errors=\"surrogateescape\"` — which is what the OS hands back for a\n#: symlink target and what the symlink branch used to feed this matcher\n#: RAW (pair-rail land round 1, P1: the surrogate is not U+FFFD, so the\n#: second reading never fired and `/Users/<user><0xFF>zzfake/` read as the\n#: placeholder `dev`). One regex, so a new marker is added in one place.\n_PERSONAL_PATH_UNDECODABLE_RE = re.compile(\"[\\ufffd\\udc80-\\udcff]\")\n\n\n","numLines":595,"startLine":1,"totalLines":595}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012yA1yG9tGzEZKoMzv7FNhR","permission_decision"