{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LhC4EZHtvoNSR97Hee2oUN","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"Row rendering for contamination personal-path reports.\"\"\"\n2\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n3\t\n4\tdef _emit_personal_path_rows(scan: PersonalPathScan,\n5\t                             allowlist: Dict[str, \"PersonalPathRow\"]) -> int:\n6\t    \"\"\"`--emit-personal-path-rows`: a row per file that is not waived TODAY.\n7\t\n8\t    A convenience for the human who must then write each reason — the emitted\n9\t    reason is `REPLACE-WITH-REASON`, which the parser REFUSES, so a paste that\n10\t    skips the decision fails the gate instead of passing it.\n11\t\n12\t    It emits for a file whose row exists but whose DIGEST no longer matches,\n13\t    because that is the ordinary workflow now: the file was edited, the\n14\t    waiver lapsed, and what the operator needs is the new digest. The reason\n15\t    still comes out unreasoned — a re-approval is a decision too.\n16\t\n17\t    A hit with no digest is a NAME hit, and no row can waive a name. Those\n18\t    are COUNTED on stderr instead of printed as rows, because stdout here\n19\t    exists to be pasted into the allowlist and a row that the parser\n20\t    refuses by construction is not something to paste.\n21\t    \"\"\"\n22\t    nameless = 0\n23\t    unexpressible = 0\n24\t    for hit in scan.hits:\n25\t        # The NAME first, in the SAME order the verdict uses it — nothing\n26\t        # waives a path. This ordering was a cure when a rule waiver was\n27\t        # consulted ahead of it and swallowed a mixed hit (pair-rail\n28\t        # round 1, P2, both lanes); the waiver is gone, and the ordering\n29\t        # stays because a mixed hit HAS a digest, so \"no digest\" was\n30\t        # never the full test for a name hit.\n31\t        if (hit.sha256 is None\n32\t                or _PERSONAL_PATH_NAME_LINE in hit.lines):\n33\t            nameless += 1\n34\t            continue\n35\t        row = allowlist.get(hit.rel)\n36\t        if row is not None and row.sha256 == hit.sha256:\n37\t            continue\n38\t        # The row is built from the path the PARSER will read, not from\n39\t        # the path the REPORT would print. `_personal_path_safe` is the\n40\t        # display choke point and, to stay injective, it doubles a\n41\t        # backslash: `docs/a\\b.md` is a legal git path and came out of\n42\t        # it as `docs/a\\\\b.md`, which fails the probe below and was\n43\t        # counted \"unexpressible\" -- with a note blaming whitespace --\n44\t        # while a HAND-WRITTEN row for that same path parses and waives\n45\t        # (mechanism lane, round 18; reproduced). Display escaping is\n46\t        # not row grammar.\n47\t        #\n48\t        # The two properties that boundary owned here are kept as\n49\t        # explicit refusals, because both are real: a path that NAMES a\n50\t        # home directory must not be printed at all (round cure-2, P2),\n51\t        # and a path carrying a control character cannot be offered as\n52\t        # a row -- escaped it is a DIFFERENT path to the parser, and\n53\t        # raw it would put a terminal command in the output. Such a\n54\t        # file has to be RENAMED, not waived, which is what the note\n55\t        # below says.\n56\t        if (_personal_path_hit_either(hit.rel)\n57\t                or not _personal_path_renders_as_itself(hit.rel)):\n58\t            unexpressible += 1\n59\t            continue\n60\t        candidate = (\"%s | sha256:%s | %s\"\n61\t                     % (hit.rel, hit.sha256, _PERSONAL_PATH_UNREASONED))\n62\t        # The emitter is judged by the PARSER, not by its own formatting.\n63\t        # `docs/frozen log.md` is a valid git path and an INVALID row:\n64\t        # `_PERSONAL_PATH_ROW_RE` refuses whitespace and `|` in the path\n65\t        # field, so the advertised paste produced a MALFORMED row and the\n66\t        # frozen artifact ended with no usable waiver at all, from a mode\n67\t        # that had exited 0 (mechanism lane, round 11; reproduced).\n68\t        # Producer and consumer share one grammar: the row is offered only\n69\t        # if the parser that will read it accepts it AND resolves it back\n70\t        # to the same path.\n71\t        probe = _PERSONAL_PATH_ROW_RE.match(candidate)\n72\t        if probe is None or probe.group(1) != hit.rel:\n73\t            unexpressible += 1\n74\t            continue\n75\t        print(candidate)\n76\t    if nameless:\n77\t        print(\n78\t            \"NOTE: %d file(s) hit on their own PATH and have no row form: \"\n79\t            \"rename them\" % nameless, file=sys.stderr)\n80\t    if unexpressible:\n81\t        print(\n82\t            \"NOTE: %d file(s) have a path this rule cannot offer as a row \"\n83\t            \"(whitespace, `|`, a control character, or a home directory \"\n84\t            \"written into the path itself) and have no row form: rename \"\n85\t            \"them — a row emitted for such a path is one the parser \"\n86\t            \"refuses, or one no reviewer could read\"\n87\t            % unexpressible, file=sys.stderr)\n88\t    return 0\n89\t\n90\t\n91\tdef _personal_path_mask_owner(match, replacement: str) -> str:\n92\t    \"\"\"``replacement`` for this owner segment -- unless it is a PLACEHOLDER.\n93\t\n94\t    The mask asks the SAME question the matcher asks: is this lowercased\n95\t    owner segment on `_PERSONAL_PATH_PLACEHOLDERS`? `alice`, `bob`,\n96\t    `devuser`, `runner` are the segments the rule exists NOT to flag, and\n97\t    replacing them told a reviewer nothing while making two different\n98\t    tracked files -- and a real owner -- render as one line (mechanism\n99\t    lane, round 24; reproduced before it was believed).\n100\t\n101\t    The RAW lowercased segment decides, and only it. NFKC folding can turn\n102\t    a full-width owner into an ASCII placeholder, never a placeholder into\n103\t    a person, so consulting the folded reading here is the bypass and not\n104\t    the safeguard -- the same asymmetry `_personal_path_hit_either` states\n105\t    from the other side.\n106\t\n107\t    `match.group(0)` is returned verbatim rather than re-spelled: the point\n108\t    is that the reader can tell WHICH file, and `/Users//<user>` and\n109\t    `-Users--<user>` are not the same path as their normal forms. A\n110\t    function replacement is used, so what is returned is taken literally --\n111\t    no backslash re-interpretation on a path that carries one.\n112\t    \"\"\"\n113\t    if match.group(1).lower() in _PERSONAL_PATH_PLACEHOLDERS:\n114\t        return match.group(0)\n115\t    return replacement\n116\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50141584-ed08-437c-bb91-f56d610ea253","uuid":"f908e726-f581-424b-b6ee-6d334f49abbc","timestamp":"2026-11-13T18:36:37.056Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_contamination/personal_path_rows.py","content":"\"\"\"Row rendering for contamination personal-path reports.\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\ndef _emit_personal_path_rows(scan: PersonalPathScan,\n                             allowlist: Dict[str, \"PersonalPathRow\"]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"`--emit-personal-path-rows`: a row per file that is not waived TODAY.\n\n    A convenience for the human who must then write each reason — the emitted\n    reason is `REPLACE-WITH-REASON`, which the parser REFUSES, so a paste that\n    skips the decision fails the gate instead of passing it.\n\n    It emits for a file whose row exists but whose DIGEST no longer matches,\n    because that is the ordinary workflow now: the file was edited, the\n    waiver lapsed, and what the operator needs is the new digest. The reason\n    still comes out unreasoned — a re-approval is a decision too.\n\n    A hit with no digest is a NAME hit, and no row can waive a name. Those\n    are COUNTED on stderr instead of printed as rows, because stdout here\n    exists to be pasted into the allowlist and a row that the parser\n    refuses by construction is not something to paste.\n    \"\"\"\n    nameless = 0\n    unexpressible = 0\n    for hit in scan.hits:\n        # The NAME first, in the SAME order the verdict uses it — nothing\n        # waives a path. This ordering was a cure when a rule waiver was\n        # consulted ahead of it and swallowed a mixed hit (pair-rail\n        # round 1, P2, both lanes); the waiver is gone, and the ordering\n        # stays because a mixed hit HAS a digest, so \"no digest\" was\n        # never the full test for a name hit.\n        if (hit.sha256 is None\n                or _PERSONAL_PATH_NAME_LINE in hit.lines):\n            nameless += 1\n            continue\n        row = allowlist.get(hit.rel)\n        if row is not None and row.sha256 == hit.sha256:\n            continue\n        # The row is built from the path the PARSER will read, not from\n        # the path the REPORT would print. `_personal_path_safe` is the\n        # display choke point and, to stay injective, it doubles a\n        # backslash: `docs/a\\b.md` is a legal git path and came out of\n        # it as `docs/a\\\\b.md`, which fails the probe below and was\n        # counted \"unexpressible\" -- with a note blaming whitespace --\n        # while a HAND-WRITTEN row for that same path parses and waives\n        # (mechanism lane, round 18; reproduced). Display escaping is\n        # not row grammar.\n        #\n        # The two properties that boundary owned here are kept as\n        # explicit refusals, because both are real: a path that NAMES a\n        # home directory must not be printed at all (round cure-2, P2),\n        # and a path carrying a control character cannot be offered as\n        # a row -- escaped it is a DIFFERENT path to the parser, and\n        # raw it would put a terminal command in the output. Such a\n        # file has to be RENAMED, not waived, which is what the note\n        # below says.\n        if (_personal_path_hit_either(hit.rel)\n                or not _personal_path_renders_as_itself(hit.rel)):\n            unexpressible += 1\n            continue\n        candidate = (\"%s | sha256:%s | %s\"\n                     % (hit.rel, hit.sha256, _PERSONAL_PATH_UNREASONED))\n        # The emitter is judged by the PARSER, not by its own formatting.\n        # `docs/frozen log.md` is a valid git path and an INVALID row:\n        # `_PERSONAL_PATH_ROW_RE` refuses whitespace and `|` in the path\n        # field, so the advertised paste produced a MALFORMED row and the\n        # frozen artifact ended with no usable waiver at all, from a mode\n        # that had exited 0 (mechanism lane, round 11; reproduced).\n        # Producer and consumer share one grammar: the row is offered only\n        # if the parser that will read it accepts it AND resolves it back\n        # to the same path.\n        probe = _PERSONAL_PATH_ROW_RE.match(candidate)\n        if probe is None or probe.group(1) != hit.rel:\n            unexpressible += 1\n            continue\n        print(candidate)\n    if nameless:\n        print(\n            \"NOTE: %d file(s) hit on their own PATH and have no row form: \"\n            \"rename them\" % nameless, file=sys.stderr)\n    if unexpressible:\n        print(\n            \"NOTE: %d file(s) have a path this rule cannot offer as a row \"\n            \"(whitespace, `|`, a control character, or a home directory \"\n            \"written into the path itself) and have no row form: rename \"\n            \"them — a row emitted for such a path is one the parser \"\n            \"refuses, or one no reviewer could read\"\n            % unexpressible, file=sys.stderr)\n    return 0\n\n\ndef _personal_path_mask_owner(match, replacement: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"``replacement`` for this owner segment -- unless it is a PLACEHOLDER.\n\n    The mask asks the SAME question the matcher asks: is this lowercased\n    owner segment on `_PERSONAL_PATH_PLACEHOLDERS`? `alice`, `bob`,\n    `devuser`, `runner` are the segments the rule exists NOT to flag, and\n    replacing them told a reviewer nothing while making two different\n    tracked files -- and a real owner -- render as one line (mechanism\n    lane, round 24; reproduced before it was believed).\n\n    The RAW lowercased segment decides, and only it. NFKC folding can turn\n    a full-width owner into an ASCII placeholder, never a placeholder into\n    a person, so consulting the folded reading here is the bypass and not\n    the safeguard -- the same asymmetry `_personal_path_hit_either` states\n    from the other side.\n\n    `match.group(0)` is returned verbatim rather than re-spelled: the point\n    is that the reader can tell WHICH file, and `/Users//<user>` and\n    `-Users--<user>` are not the same path as their normal forms. A\n    function replacement is used, so what is returned is taken literally --\n    no backslash re-interpretation on a path that carries one.\n    \"\"\"\n    if match.group(1).lower() in _PERSONAL_PATH_PLACEHOLDERS:\n        return match.group(0)\n    return replacement\n","numLines":116,"startLine":1,"totalLines":116}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LhC4EZHtvoNSR97Hee2oUN","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
