{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014AoBrwgPwCK2qNRipDhxko","type":"tool_result","content":"44-  version floor, without a matching hash (Security and governance, below).\n45-- The secret redactor that the Codex and Grok egress uses no longer cuts\n46-  the text at offsets taken from its NFKC form, a defect present since\n47:  v1.0.0. Its `scan()` offsets now index the text passed in (Fixed,\n48-  below).\n49-- The kernel's hard-deny list covers the code that moved out of seven\n50-  kernel entry points, five flat modules that kernel code calls, and the\n--\n253-\n254-### Fixed — the secret redactor's offsets after an NFKC-changing character (present since v1.0.0)\n255-\n256:- `scan()` in `_lib/secret_patterns.py` matched the NFKC-normalized view\n257-  of a text but reported offsets in that view, while `redact()` and\n258-  `scan_and_redact()` cut the original text with them. A character whose\n259-  NFKC form has another length (an ellipsis, a ligature) before a secret\n--\n261-  label. The Codex and Grok egress redactor (`_lib/codex_egress_redact.py`)\n262-  uses these functions. Every release since v1.0.0 has the defect. The\n263-  signed v1.4.0 verdict declared this defect as its condition 58.\n264:- `scan()` now reports offsets in the text passed in. It matches that text\n265-  and its NFKC view, maps the view's matches back, and merges overlapping\n266-  findings into one span. The output is the original text with only those\n267-  spans replaced; no normalized character reaches it.\n--\n474-  no longer unlocks them; they need `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE` with\n475-  `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK=I-ACCEPT`.\n476-- If your code reads `Finding.start` and `Finding.end` from\n477:  `secret_patterns.scan()`, they now index the text you passed in, not its\n478-  NFKC form.\n479-- Expect a yellow `main_checkout_state` in `/ceo-boot` when your main\n480-  checkout is on a feature branch.\n--\n610-\n611-- `check_cost_envelope.py` passed `action` twice to its emitter, in every\n612-  release since v1.0.0. The resulting `TypeError` was swallowed by the\n613:  fail-open path: the block happened, but no event reached the log.\n614-- A block now writes exactly one `cost_envelope_capped` line, with an HMAC.\n615-  The line carries only the fields on the event's allowlist, `project`\n616-  among them. The block itself does not change.\n--\n793-  - Breaks the race already wrote stay in the live log and in the rotated\n794-    archives: this release rewrites no history.\n795-  - Under lock saturation, the longer critical section can time out and\n796:    drop the line (fail-open, breadcrumb only).\n797-  - After a failed marker write, the next line another writer signs chains\n798-    to the missing marker, and `verify_chain()` reports a break there.\n799-  - `append_entry` does not increment the `audit-log.chain-length` counter,\n--\n1932-\n1933-### Added — compaction continuity and work-boundary persistence (PLAN-179, ADR-195)\n1934-\n1935:Two new hooks, both **advisory, both fail-open, neither blocks a tool\n1936-call today**:\n1937-\n1938-- **`check_compact_pinning.py`** (`SessionStart`, matcher `compact`):\n--\n2171-- The canonical-edit matcher now enforces a **per-invocation wall\n2172-  deadline that fails CLOSED** (`_HOOK_WALL_BUDGET_S`, injectable clock),\n2173-  replacing the unbounded sentinel sweep — and the published\n2174:  fail-open-on-infrastructure contract in `CLAUDE.md` §4 / `AGENTS.md` §1\n2175-  now names this exception explicitly: a timeout *inside the matcher* is\n2176-  an incomplete verification, not infrastructure. Recovery route:\n2177-  provenance-pinned unlock (`CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK` +\n--\n2179-- Sentinel-verification **cache partitioned** (signature validity no\n2180-  longer keyed per-target). This closes the measured saturation window —\n2181-  4.16 s consumed of a 5 s budget at 20 candidate paths — that made the\n2182:  old fail-open deadline attacker-reachable by planting sentinels\n2183-  (ADR-164-AMEND-1). A correctness/security fix; no throughput claim.\n2184-- Deadline blocks are **countable in the chain**: the veto breadcrumb\n2185-  carries `reason_code=canonical_edit_hook_fault` instead of\n--\n2357-the substrate work that made that possible. As always: governance and\n2358-auditability — no speed claim.\n2359-\n2360:### Fixed — the pair-rail was 100% fail-open (PLAN-164, ADR-110-AMEND-1)\n2361-\n2362-- **`CEO_PAIR_RAIL_TIMEOUT_S` internal default 30 → 120 s**, and the\n2363-  `check_pair_rail.py` PreToolUse **registration timeout 60 → 150 s** in both\n--\n\n[Showing results with pagination = limit: 80]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50141584-ed08-437c-bb91-f56d610ea253","uuid":"72dbc550-7559-4df5-9f65-a64f620499b0","timestamp":"2026-11-13T18:39:45.391Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"44-  version floor, without a matching hash (Security and governance, below).\n45-- The secret redactor that the Codex and Grok egress uses no longer cuts\n46-  the text at offsets taken from its NFKC form, a defect present since\n47:  v1.0.0. Its `scan()` offsets now index the text passed in (Fixed,\n48-  below).\n49-- The kernel's hard-deny list covers the code that moved out of seven\n50-  kernel entry points, five flat modules that kernel code calls, and the\n--\n253-\n254-### Fixed — the secret redactor's offsets after an NFKC-changing character (present since v1.0.0)\n255-\n256:- `scan()` in `_lib/secret_patterns.py` matched the NFKC-normalized view\n257-  of a text but reported offsets in that view, while `redact()` and\n258-  `scan_and_redact()` cut the original text with them. A character whose\n259-  NFKC form has another length (an ellipsis, a ligature) before a secret\n--\n261-  label. The Codex and Grok egress redactor (`_lib/codex_egress_redact.py`)\n262-  uses these functions. Every release since v1.0.0 has the defect. The\n263-  signed v1.4.0 verdict declared this defect as its co